// Full-length practice tests (original texts; names and figures invented)
window.IELTS_TESTS = [{"id":"rt1","kind":"reading","title":"Reading test 1","passages":[{"title":"The frogs that wait for rain","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"Across the dry interior of the Merrin Basin, a region where rain may fail to fall for several years in succession, the landscape appears almost lifeless for most of the time. Yet within hours of a heavy storm, the clay pans that dot the plain fill with water and come alive with the calls of thousands of frogs. For a long time, settlers assumed that the animals had somehow travelled in from wetter country, carried by floodwater or arriving overland during the night. In fact, they had been there all along, sealed beneath the ground in a state of suspended life. The clay-pan frog, as the species is commonly known, has since become one of the most closely studied examples of how a vertebrate can survive extreme and prolonged drought."},{"label":"B","text":"The frog's survival strategy begins while the pools are still shrinking. Rather than waiting until the surface has baked hard, it starts to dig while the mud at the bottom of the pool is still damp, typically choosing the spot where water lingered longest and the sediment remains soft. It burrows backwards, pushing the soil aside with hard, spade-shaped projections on its hind feet, and gradually rotates its body so that it sinks downwards in a slow spiral. Depending on the texture of the soil, it may come to rest anywhere from a few centimetres to almost a metre below the surface, in a small chamber that it shapes by pressing its body firmly against the walls."},{"label":"C","text":"Once settled, the frog undergoes a remarkable transformation. Its skin begins to shed, as it does periodically throughout the animal's life, but instead of being sloughed off and left behind, each old layer remains attached to the body and dries into a thin, transparent film. Over a period of weeks, dozens of these layers accumulate to form a cocoon that encloses the frog entirely except for two small openings at the nostrils, through which it continues to breathe. The cocoon dramatically reduces the amount of moisture lost through the skin, which in most amphibians is highly permeable. In addition, before burrowing, the frog fills its bladder with dilute urine, which can amount to a considerable proportion of its body weight. During the dry months this reserve is slowly reabsorbed into the body, helping to keep its tissues hydrated."},{"label":"D","text":"Equally important is the frog's ability to slow its body down. In the dormant state known as aestivation, its metabolic rate falls to a small fraction of the normal resting level, and its heart beats only a few times each minute. With almost no food entering its body, the animal draws on fat stored in its abdomen, using it so economically that a single season's reserves may last for years. What has puzzled physiologists most, however, is the condition of the frog's muscles. In humans and most other animals, long periods of inactivity cause muscles to waste away, yet the clay-pan frog emerges from its burrow able to hop, swim and call almost immediately. Several explanations have been proposed, including the production of protective compounds that prevent tissue from breaking down, but none has yet been confirmed."},{"label":"E","text":"The end of dormancy is triggered by water itself. When heavy rain soaks deep into the ground, moisture seeps into the chamber and softens the cocoon. The frog then pulls the loosened material away from its body with its front legs and eats it, a habit that allows it to recover some of the protein it invested in building the covering. Within a few hours it has dug its way back to the surface, usually emerging at night, when cooler air reduces the risk of drying out."},{"label":"F","text":"What follows is a race against time. The pools created by a storm may last only a few weeks, so the frogs must breed almost at once. Males gather at the water's edge and call through the night, and females lay their eggs within days of emerging. The tadpoles that hatch develop at an exceptional speed, and when food is scarce, some of them turn to feeding on their siblings, a grim strategy that allows at least a few individuals to grow fast enough to leave the water before it disappears. The young frogs that survive then gorge on insects to build up fat reserves before digging their first burrows."},{"label":"G","text":"The species has attracted interest well beyond the field of zoology. Medical scientists hope that understanding how the frog protects its muscles might one day help people who face similar problems, such as astronauts on long missions and patients confined to bed. At the same time, ecologists warn that the frog's finely tuned life cycle may leave it vulnerable to changes in rainfall. A light shower that wets only the upper layers of soil is unlikely to wake it, but a moderate storm may bring the animals to the surface only for the resulting pools to evaporate before the tadpoles are fully developed. How long an individual can survive underground remains uncertain: some are believed to have stayed buried for several years, but no researcher has yet been able to follow a single frog through an entire drought."}],"groups":[{"type":"tfng","instructions":"Questions 1–5. Do the following statements agree with the information given in Reading Passage 1? Choose TRUE if the statement agrees with the information, FALSE if the statement contradicts the information, or NOT GIVEN if there is no information on this.","questions":[{"q":"The frog begins digging before the floor of the pool has become hard.","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"TRUE","explanation":"B: rather than waiting until the surface has baked hard, it starts to dig while the mud is still damp."},{"q":"The cocoon of an older frog contains more layers than that of a younger frog.","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"NOT GIVEN","explanation":"C says dozens of layers build up over weeks, but nothing compares older and younger frogs."},{"q":"During aestivation, the frog's heart stops beating for long periods.","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"FALSE","explanation":"D: its heart beats only a few times each minute, so it keeps beating."},{"q":"Scientists have not yet established why the frog's muscles remain in good condition.","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"TRUE","explanation":"D: several explanations have been proposed, but none has yet been confirmed."},{"q":"Researchers have tracked individual frogs throughout a complete drought.","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"FALSE","explanation":"G: no researcher has yet been able to follow a single frog through an entire drought."}]},{"type":"diag","instructions":"Questions 6–9. Complete the flow-chart. Choose ONE WORD ONLY from the passage for each answer.","outline":{"style":"flowchart","rows":[["As the pool shrinks, the frog digs backwards using spade-shaped projections on its hind [1]"],["Old layers of [2] stay on the body and dry into a protective cocoon"],["Water stored in the [3] is gradually taken back into the body"],["Heavy rain reaches the chamber and softens the cocoon"],["The frog removes the loosened covering and [4] it to regain protein, then returns to the surface"]]},"questions":[{"q":"Gap 1","answer":"feet","explanation":"B: hard, spade-shaped projections on its hind feet."},{"q":"Gap 2","answer":"skin","explanation":"C: its skin begins to shed, and each old layer remains attached and dries into a film."},{"q":"Gap 3","answer":"bladder","explanation":"C: the frog fills its bladder with dilute urine, which is slowly reabsorbed."},{"q":"Gap 4","answer":"eats","explanation":"E: pulls the loosened material away and eats it, recovering some of the protein."}]},{"type":"sent","instructions":"Questions 10–13. Complete the sentences below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.","questions":[{"q":"When there is little food, some tadpoles speed up their growth by eating their ____.","answer":"siblings","explanation":"F: some of them turn to feeding on their siblings."},{"q":"Before burrowing for the first time, young frogs eat large numbers of insects to build up ____.","answer":"fat reserves","alt":["reserves"],"explanation":"F: gorge on insects to build up fat reserves."},{"q":"Research on the frog's muscles might eventually benefit ____ on long missions.","answer":"astronauts","explanation":"G: astronauts on long missions and patients confined to bed."},{"q":"After a moderate storm, the pools may ____ before the tadpoles have finished developing.","answer":"evaporate","explanation":"G: the resulting pools evaporate before the tadpoles are fully developed. A light shower would not wake the frogs at all."}]}]},{"title":"Signals across the hills","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"For most of human history, a message could travel no faster than the person or animal carrying it. A rider on a good horse, changing mounts at regular stations, might cover a long distance in a day, but news of a distant battle or a failed harvest could still take a week or more to reach a capital city. It is therefore easy to understand the astonishment that greeted a new system unveiled in the Kingdom of Velmora in the final decade of the eighteenth century. Using nothing more than wooden towers, telescopes and trained observers, it could carry a short message from the coast to the capital, a distance of several hundred kilometres, in well under an hour. Long before the age of electricity, Velmora had built what was, in effect, a national communications network."},{"label":"B","text":"The principle was simple. Towers were built on hilltops or church roofs, usually between ten and fifteen kilometres apart, so that each was visible from the next. On top of each stood a tall mast carrying a pivoting crossbeam, at either end of which hung a smaller movable arm. Operators inside the tower used ropes and pulleys to set the beam and arms in a particular combination of angles, and each combination stood for a number. An operator at the next tower would watch through a telescope, copy the position on his own apparatus, and so pass it on down the line. The numbers referred to entries in a codebook containing thousands of letters, words and common phrases. For the historian Agnes Thorley, this codebook was the system's real achievement. 'The arms were simple carpentry,' she writes. 'The genius lay in reducing language to a set of numbers that could be sent by anyone.'"},{"label":"C","text":"The people who worked the towers were, in fact, deliberately kept from understanding what they sent. Most were former soldiers, chosen for their discipline and their eyesight rather than their education, and they were paid little for long hours spent staring at a distant hillside. Only the directors at each end of a line held the full codebook; the operators in between were taught merely to reproduce the positions they observed and to record them in a logbook. This arrangement protected the secrecy of government messages, since a bribed or careless operator could reveal nothing of their content, but it also meant that the men in the towers had no way of noticing when a signal made no sense."},{"label":"D","text":"Nor could the system escape the limits imposed by nature. Signals could only be read in daylight and with clear visibility, so fog, heavy rain or snow brought the entire line to a halt, and nothing at all could be sent at night. Errors were another weakness: because each operator copied the one before, a single misreading could be passed along the chain and repeated at every tower that followed. The historian Rafael Ostrowski, who studied the surviving logbooks of the northern line, argues that the system's famous speed is something of a myth. A message might race across the country in forty minutes on a bright spring morning, he points out, but if it was handed in on a misty winter afternoon it could wait until the next day, or longer, before it moved at all."},{"label":"E","text":"From the beginning, the network served the state alone. It carried military orders, reports from the frontier and, in later years, official announcements such as the results of the national lottery. Private citizens and merchants repeatedly asked to use it and were refused, a policy that the cultural historian Mira Delacourt believes did much to shape public attitudes. In her view, a network that ordinary people could see but never use quickly became a symbol of distant and secretive authority, and the towers appear in popular songs of the period as objects of suspicion rather than pride. Exclusion also created opportunities for fraud. In one notorious case, two merchants paid an operator to insert small, deliberate errors into official messages, allowing them to learn about movements in government bond prices before their rivals."},{"label":"F","text":"The decline of the towers, when it came, was swift. The electric telegraph, introduced into Velmora around the middle of the nineteenth century, could operate at night and in any weather, required far fewer staff and could be opened to paying customers. Within about twenty years the last optical lines had closed. Some towers were demolished for their stone, others were converted into houses or farm buildings, and many were simply left to fall into ruin on the hilltops where they had once stood."},{"label":"G","text":"Historians remain divided over how much the system really mattered. Thorley regards it as the first genuine telecommunications network, arguing that its methods for checking errors and managing the flow of messages were later adopted, often with little change, by the engineers who built the electric telegraph. Ostrowski is more sceptical. The volume of messages was tiny, he notes, and their effect was mainly to strengthen the hand of the central government rather than to transform the lives of ordinary citizens. Yet even he concedes that the towers changed expectations. Once people had seen news cross a country in an hour, the idea that information should move almost instantly no longer seemed fantastic."}],"groups":[{"type":"mhead","instructions":"Questions 14–20. Reading Passage 2 has seven paragraphs, A–G. Choose the correct heading for each paragraph from the list of headings below. There are more headings than paragraphs.","options":["i A network closed to ordinary users and exploited by a few","ii The mechanics of relaying a signal","iii Staff who were prevented from understanding their work","iv Disagreement about the system's true importance","v A method faster than anything that came before","vi At the mercy of conditions beyond human control","vii The spread of the towers to neighbouring countries","viii Replaced by a more practical invention","ix How operators were trained to read the codebook"],"questions":[{"q":"Paragraph A","answer":"v","explanation":"A: messages had travelled no faster than a horse; the towers carried a message hundreds of kilometres in under an hour."},{"q":"Paragraph B","answer":"ii","explanation":"B: how the beam and arms were set, observed through a telescope and copied to the next tower."},{"q":"Paragraph C","answer":"iii","explanation":"C: operators were deliberately kept from understanding what they sent. Heading ix is wrong: they never held the codebook."},{"q":"Paragraph D","answer":"vi","explanation":"D: fog, rain, snow and darkness halted the line (the limits imposed by nature)."},{"q":"Paragraph E","answer":"i","explanation":"E: the network served the state alone, refused private users, and was used for fraud by two merchants."},{"q":"Paragraph F","answer":"viii","explanation":"F: the electric telegraph worked at night and in any weather, and the optical lines closed."},{"q":"Paragraph G","answer":"iv","explanation":"G: historians remain divided over how much the system mattered. Heading vii is never mentioned."}]},{"type":"mfeat","instructions":"Questions 21–24. Look at the following statements and the list of historians below. Match each statement with the correct historian, A, B or C. You may use any letter more than once.","options":["A Agnes Thorley","B Rafael Ostrowski","C Mira Delacourt"],"questions":[{"q":"The way language was converted into numbers was more impressive than the equipment.","answer":"A","explanation":"B: Thorley: 'The arms were simple carpentry ... the genius lay in reducing language to a set of numbers.'"},{"q":"Accounts of the system's speed overlook the time that messages spent waiting.","answer":"B","explanation":"D: Ostrowski says the famous speed is a myth because a message could wait until the next day before it moved."},{"q":"Refusing access to the public made people regard the network with mistrust.","answer":"C","explanation":"E: Delacourt: the towers became a symbol of secretive authority and objects of suspicion."},{"q":"Some of the system's procedures were taken over by those who developed a later technology.","answer":"A","explanation":"G: Thorley argues its methods for checking errors and managing messages were adopted by the builders of the electric telegraph."}]},{"type":"mcq2","instructions":"Questions 25–26. Choose TWO letters, A–E.","questions":[{"q":"Which TWO weaknesses of the tower system are mentioned in the passage?","options":["A It could not be used after dark.","B Operators often lost their codebooks.","C A mistake could be copied from tower to tower.","D The towers were expensive to build and repair.","E Operators frequently abandoned their posts in winter."],"answer":["A","C"],"explanation":"D: nothing at all could be sent at night; a single misreading could be passed along the chain. Option B is wrong because paragraph C says operators never held the codebook; cost and abandoned posts are not mentioned."}]}]},{"title":"Should learning feel easy?","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"Ask a group of students what makes a good lesson and many will give a similar answer: the teacher explained things clearly, the ideas fell neatly into place, and the work felt manageable. Few would praise a lesson in which they spent half an hour confused. This preference is natural, and it is shared by many teachers, parents and designers of educational software, all of whom tend to assume that the smoother the learning process, the more effective it must be. I want to argue that this assumption, though understandable, is frequently mistaken, and that some of the conditions which make learning feel harder are precisely the ones that make it last."},{"label":"B","text":"Psychologists have a name for such conditions: desirable difficulties. They include spacing practice out over several days rather than cramming it into a single session, mixing different types of problem instead of practising one type at a time, and testing oneself rather than simply rereading notes. Each of these slows apparent progress. Yet a growing body of research suggests that they improve the ability to remember and apply material weeks or months later. Rereading is a revealing example. It remains one of the most popular revision methods, largely because it feels productive: as the text becomes familiar, students sense that they are mastering it. But recognising something on the page is not the same as being able to recall it in an examination hall, and that comfortable sense of familiarity can be dangerously misleading."},{"label":"C","text":"A study conducted by a research team at the Halden Institute illustrates the point. Two groups of fifteen-year-olds were taught a new topic in statistics. The first group received a clear explanation followed by practice exercises. The second was given challenging problems before any instruction, struggled with them for some time, and only then received the same explanation. On a test taken immediately afterwards, the two groups performed at a similar level. When they were tested again a month later, however, those who had struggled first did noticeably better, particularly on questions that differed from the ones they had practised. Tellingly, the same students had rated their lessons as less enjoyable and had judged themselves to be less capable than their peers."},{"label":"D","text":"That final detail goes to the heart of the problem. Learners are poor judges of their own learning, because they tend to measure it by how it feels at the time. Teachers face the same trap. In many schools and universities, teaching is evaluated partly through student satisfaction surveys, and a teacher whose lessons leave students feeling puzzled is likely to receive lower ratings than one whose lessons feel effortless. It would be unfair, in my view, to blame teachers who respond to this by making their lessons as smooth as possible; they are behaving quite sensibly given the signals they receive. The fault lies with a system that rewards the appearance of learning rather than learning itself."},{"label":"E","text":"None of this means that difficulty is always beneficial, and critics of the approach raise objections that deserve a serious answer. They point out that students who struggle repeatedly may become disheartened, particularly those who already lack confidence, and that confusion can increase anxiety to the point where little is learned at all. Beginners, they argue, need clear guidance, not puzzles. I think these critics are partly right. There is a crucial difference between difficulty that is desirable and difficulty that is merely bewildering. Struggle seems to help only when learners have enough prior knowledge to make a genuine attempt at the problem; a student who has no idea where to begin is not engaged in productive effort, but is simply lost."},{"label":"F","text":"What, then, should teachers do? First, they can design tasks that lie just beyond students' present level of skill, demanding enough to require real effort but not so hard as to be hopeless. Second, struggle should always be followed by a clear explanation, so that students can connect their own attempts to the correct method. Third, and perhaps most importantly, students should be told why they are being asked to struggle. Learners who understand that confusion is a normal part of learning are far less likely to interpret it as a sign of failure. Beyond the classroom, I believe institutions should stop relying on satisfaction surveys alone when they assess the quality of teaching, and should look also at what students can still do months after a course has ended."},{"label":"G","text":"I am not arguing that learning should be made painful for its own sake. The aim is not suffering but durable understanding, and a good teacher will still explain clearly and encourage generously. But we should be wary of anything that promises effortless progress. The current enthusiasm for learning applications that guarantee rapid results with minimal effort strikes me as misplaced: if the research on desirable difficulties is even partly correct, the very ease that makes such products attractive may be exactly what limits their value. Learning that feels easy is not necessarily learning that has failed, but it deserves a second look."}],"groups":[{"type":"mcq","instructions":"Questions 27–30. Choose the correct letter, A, B, C or D.","questions":[{"q":"According to the writer, rereading notes is a popular revision method because","options":["A it takes less time than other methods.","B it produces a sense of familiarity that students mistake for knowledge.","C most teachers recommend it to their students.","D it leads to better results in examinations."],"answer":"B","explanation":"B: it feels productive as the text becomes familiar, but recognising is not the same as recalling."},{"q":"In the Halden Institute study, the students who attempted problems before receiving an explanation","options":["A scored higher than the other group on the test taken straight after the lesson.","B found their lessons more enjoyable than the other group did.","C performed better than the other group when tested a month later.","D needed a longer explanation than the other group."],"answer":"C","explanation":"C: a month later, those who had struggled first did noticeably better. Immediately afterwards both groups performed at a similar level, and they enjoyed the lessons less."},{"q":"What point does the writer make about student satisfaction surveys?","options":["A They discourage teachers from making learning difficult.","B They are rarely taken seriously by institutions.","C They should be abolished in schools and universities.","D They give an accurate picture of how much students learn."],"answer":"A","explanation":"D: a teacher whose lessons leave students puzzled gets lower ratings, so teachers make lessons smooth. In F the writer says surveys should not be used alone, not abolished."},{"q":"What is the writer's main purpose in the passage?","options":["A to reject the use of clear explanations in teaching","B to argue that certain kinds of difficulty make learning more effective","C to describe a new way of assessing teachers","D to show that weaker students learn more slowly than others"],"answer":"B","explanation":"A and G: conditions that make learning feel harder can make it last; the writer still wants clear explanation (G), so A is wrong."}]},{"type":"ynng","instructions":"Questions 31–35. Do the following statements agree with the claims of the writer in Reading Passage 3? Choose YES if the statement agrees with the claims of the writer, NO if the statement contradicts the claims of the writer, or NOT GIVEN if it is impossible to say what the writer thinks about this.","questions":[{"q":"Students are generally able to judge accurately how much they have learned.","options":["YES","NO","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"NO","explanation":"D: learners are poor judges of their own learning, because they measure it by how it feels."},{"q":"The concerns raised by opponents of deliberate difficulty are partly justified.","options":["YES","NO","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"YES","explanation":"E: 'I think these critics are partly right.'"},{"q":"Learning through struggle is more effective in mathematics than in language subjects.","options":["YES","NO","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"NOT GIVEN","explanation":"The writer never compares school subjects; only one statistics study is described."},{"q":"The quality of teaching should be judged partly by what students remember long after a course.","options":["YES","NO","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"YES","explanation":"F: institutions should look also at what students can still do months after a course has ended."},{"q":"Applications that promise quick and easy learning are likely to be highly effective.","options":["YES","NO","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"NO","explanation":"G: the enthusiasm for such applications strikes the writer as misplaced; their ease may limit their value."}]},{"type":"summ","instructions":"Questions 36–40. Complete the summary using the list of words, A–I, below.","outline":{"style":"summary","rows":[["Critics claim that making learning harder may [1] less able students and raise their level of [2]. The writer accepts that struggle is only useful when learners already have sufficient [3] to attempt a problem. Teachers should set tasks slightly beyond students' current [4], should always follow a period of struggle with clear [5], and should explain why struggling is worthwhile."]]},"options":["A discourage","B motivate","C anxiety","D confidence","E knowledge","F ability","G instruction","H praise","I curiosity"],"questions":[{"q":"Gap 1","answer":"A","explanation":"E: students who struggle repeatedly may become disheartened (= discouraged)."},{"q":"Gap 2","answer":"C","explanation":"E: confusion can increase anxiety. Confidence is what these students already lack, so D does not fit."},{"q":"Gap 3","answer":"E","explanation":"E: struggle helps only when learners have enough prior knowledge to make a genuine attempt."},{"q":"Gap 4","answer":"F","explanation":"F: tasks that lie just beyond students' present level of skill (= ability)."},{"q":"Gap 5","answer":"G","explanation":"F: struggle should always be followed by a clear explanation (= instruction). Praise is not mentioned as a follow-up."}]}]}]},{"id":"rt2","kind":"reading","title":"Reading test 2","passages":[{"title":"Bringing the bogs back to life","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"Across the cool, wet uplands of many northern regions, the ground underfoot is not really soil at all. It is peat: a dense, dark accumulation of partly decayed mosses, sedges and heathers that has built up over thousands of years. Because the plants that form it grow in permanently waterlogged conditions, there is too little oxygen for the bacteria and fungi that would normally break dead material down. Instead of rotting away, each season's growth is pressed beneath the next, and the bog rises by only a tiny amount each year. A peat layer several metres deep may therefore represent the slow work of many millennia."},{"label":"B","text":"For much of the last two centuries, such landscapes were regarded as unproductive. Landowners dug long networks of drainage ditches across them in the hope of turning wet moorland into pasture or forestry plantations, and in some districts peat was cut and dried for use as household fuel. The results were rarely what had been promised. Grazing animals found little to eat on the drained surface, most of the young conifers planted in neat rows failed to thrive, and the exposed peat began to dry, crack and blow away in the wind. In some places the surface of the bog sank noticeably as the dried material shrank and wasted away."},{"label":"C","text":"Ecologists now understand that the damage went far beyond the loss of a few rare plants. Once air reaches dried peat, the carbon locked inside it begins to combine with oxygen and escape into the atmosphere as carbon dioxide. A healthy bog removes carbon from the air; a degraded one releases it. Drained moors also behave differently in heavy rain. An intact bog acts somewhat like a sponge, holding water and releasing it gradually, whereas a network of ditches carries storm water rapidly downhill, adding to the risk of flooding in the valleys below. Water companies have noticed another consequence: rivers draining damaged peat carry brown, dissolved organic matter that is expensive to remove before the water can be supplied to homes."},{"label":"D","text":"Restoration work on the Carrow Moors, an upland plateau where several conservation groups have cooperated for over a decade, illustrates the methods now in use. The first and most important step is to block the old ditches. Teams build small dams of compacted peat or wooden boards at regular intervals along each channel, so that water collects behind them in a series of shallow pools rather than flowing away. As the water table rises, the surrounding peat is gradually rewetted, and the steady loss of carbon begins to slow."},{"label":"E","text":"Rewetting alone, however, does not always bring the bog back to life. Where the surface has been bare for many years, the next stage is to cover it with a thin layer of cut heather, which shelters the ground from wind and sun. Small plugs of sphagnum moss, grown in nurseries from fragments collected on healthy bogs, are then planted by hand into this protective layer. Sphagnum is the key species, because it can hold many times its own weight in water and creates the acidic, airless conditions in which peat forms. Once the moss has spread across the surface, new peat can begin to accumulate. In the wettest hollows, cotton grass and other sedges often return of their own accord, helping to bind the loose surface together."},{"label":"F","text":"Progress is slow, and it is not easy to measure. On the Carrow Moors, volunteers check the depth of the water table each month using narrow plastic tubes sunk into the ground, while botanists record the spread of moss within fixed squares marked out across the plateau. The early results are encouraging: within five years of the ditches being blocked, the water level in most monitored areas had risen close to the surface, and several bird species that feed on insects in wet ground had returned to breed. Yet researchers caution that a bog which took thousands of years to form cannot be rebuilt within a single human career, and that some sites may continue to release carbon for decades before they begin to store it again."},{"label":"G","text":"There are also disagreements about priorities. Some farmers worry that rewetting will reduce the land available for sheep, although in practice most restored areas were of little value for grazing. Others question whether limited conservation funds should be spent on remote uplands rather than on woodlands or rivers closer to where people live. Supporters of restoration reply that few other projects offer benefits for wildlife, water quality, flood prevention and climate at the same time, and that the cost of doing nothing is likely to grow every year that the peat continues to dry out."}],"groups":[{"type":"tfng","instructions":"Questions 1–5. Do the following statements agree with the information given in Reading Passage 1? Choose TRUE if the statement agrees with the information, FALSE if the statement contradicts the information, or NOT GIVEN if there is no information on this.","questions":[{"q":"Peat builds up because a lack of oxygen prevents dead plants from decomposing normally.","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"TRUE","explanation":"A: there is too little oxygen for the bacteria and fungi that would normally break dead material down; instead of rotting away, growth is pressed beneath the next."},{"q":"Most of the forestry plantations on drained moorland grew successfully.","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"FALSE","explanation":"B: most of the young conifers planted in neat rows failed to thrive, and the results were rarely what had been promised."},{"q":"Peat cut for fuel was mainly sold to people living in towns.","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"NOT GIVEN","explanation":"B says peat was cut and dried for use as household fuel, but nothing about who bought it or where it was sold."},{"q":"Draining a bog can make floods more likely in lower areas.","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"TRUE","explanation":"C: ditches carry storm water rapidly downhill, adding to the risk of flooding in the valleys below."},{"q":"The restoration of the Carrow Moors has been carried out by a single organisation.","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"FALSE","explanation":"D: several conservation groups have cooperated for over a decade."}]},{"type":"diag","instructions":"Questions 6–9. Complete the flow-chart below. Choose ONE WORD ONLY from the passage for each answer.","outline":{"style":"flowchart","rows":[["Old drainage ditches are blocked with small [1] of compacted peat or wooden boards"],["Water collects in a series of shallow [2], and the surrounding peat is rewetted"],["Bare ground is covered with a thin layer of cut [3] to protect it from wind and sun"],["Plugs of sphagnum moss raised in [4] are planted by hand"],["The moss spreads and new peat begins to accumulate"]]},"questions":[{"q":"Gap 1","answer":"dams","explanation":"D: teams build small dams of compacted peat or wooden boards."},{"q":"Gap 2","answer":"pools","explanation":"D: water collects behind them in a series of shallow pools."},{"q":"Gap 3","answer":"heather","explanation":"E: cover it with a thin layer of cut heather, which shelters the ground."},{"q":"Gap 4","answer":"nurseries","explanation":"E: plugs of sphagnum moss, grown in nurseries from fragments collected on healthy bogs."}]},{"type":"sent","instructions":"Questions 10–13. Complete the sentences below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.","questions":[{"q":"Each month, volunteers use ____ placed in the ground to measure the level of the water table.","answer":"plastic tubes","alt":["narrow plastic tubes","tubes"],"explanation":"F: volunteers check the depth of the water table each month using narrow plastic tubes sunk into the ground."},{"q":"Botanists monitor how far the moss has spread inside ____ on the plateau.","answer":"fixed squares","alt":["squares"],"explanation":"F: botanists record the spread of moss within fixed squares marked out across the plateau."},{"q":"Some restored sites may go on giving off carbon for ____ before they start to store it.","answer":"decades","explanation":"F: some sites may continue to release carbon for decades before they begin to store it again."},{"q":"Some farmers are concerned that restoration will leave less land for ____.","answer":"sheep","explanation":"G: some farmers worry that rewetting will reduce the land available for sheep. 'Grazing' is used later to say the land had little value, so it does not fit the sentence."}]}]},{"title":"Lines on the map: an economic history of the street grid","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"Few features of the modern city seem as ordinary as the rectangular block. Streets crossing at right angles, numbered avenues and evenly sized plots are so familiar in many places that they appear almost natural. Yet the grid is a deliberate invention, and its long history reveals as much about money and property as it does about design. Urban historians increasingly argue that to understand why cities look the way they do, one must first ask who was selling the land, to whom, and on what terms."},{"label":"B","text":"Grid plans are ancient, but their greatest expansion came with the rapid founding of new towns during periods of settlement and industrial growth. In these circumstances the grid offered a decisive practical advantage: it could be laid out quickly by surveyors who had never examined the terrain in detail. A few straight lines drawn on paper could divide an entire district into identical lots, each of which could be described in a legal document by a simple number. The historian Teodora Blasko has called the early grid 'a machine for selling land', pointing out that in many new towns lots were auctioned before a single building had been erected. Grids also simplified the work of officials who recorded ownership and collected taxes, since every plot could be located on a single master map."},{"label":"C","text":"This ease of division had important economic consequences. Because rectangular plots were interchangeable, they could be bought and sold by investors who never intended to live in the town, and land became a commodity traded in much the same way as grain or timber. Speculation drove prices upward in good years and left whole districts of empty lots when demand collapsed. Blasko notes that several towns founded in this way were half-abandoned within a generation, their streets still marked out across fields where nobody had chosen to build."},{"label":"D","text":"Critics of the grid have long complained about its indifference to landscape. A plan drawn in a distant office ignored hills, rivers and marshes, so that streets climbed slopes too steep for carts or ran straight into ground that flooded each spring. The architectural writer Joaquim Ferrell argued that this rigidity produced monotonous towns with no natural centre, in which every corner looked much like every other. He was particularly scornful of towns in which the main square had been placed by measurement alone, at the exact geometric middle of the plan, regardless of where trade and traffic actually flowed. In his view, older cities that had grown gradually along winding routes possessed a variety and a sense of place that no surveyor's ruler could ever reproduce."},{"label":"E","text":"By the late nineteenth century, such objections had begun to shape new developments. Designers of suburbs for wealthier residents introduced curving roads, quiet dead ends and irregular plots that followed the natural contours of the land. These layouts were marketed as healthier and more peaceful, offering an escape from the noise and crowding of the industrial city. Many were laid out around existing trees and streams, which were presented to buyers as part of the attraction. They were also, however, more expensive to survey and to supply with services, since water pipes and drains had to follow longer, less direct routes. The curved suburb thus became, in part, a symbol of status: only those who could afford its higher costs could enjoy its apparent naturalness."},{"label":"F","text":"The economist Lise Haugland has offered a rather different perspective on this shift. Analysing property records from a number of mid-sized towns, she found that plots on regular grids continued to change hands more frequently than those in curved suburbs, and that their prices responded more quickly to changes in the wider economy. Haugland argues that this was not a weakness but a strength: a grid allows a city to adapt, because land can easily be combined into larger sites or divided again as needs change. Curved suburbs, by contrast, tended to fix a single pattern of use for a century or more."},{"label":"G","text":"In recent decades, planners have looked at the grid with fresh appreciation. Short blocks and frequent junctions make walking more convenient, spread traffic across many routes rather than concentrating it on a few main roads, and make it easier for shops and small businesses to find customers. Developers, too, have rediscovered the advantage that first made the grid attractive: regular plots remain simple to value, to finance and to sell. Some new districts now combine a basic grid with variations that respond to the local landscape, attempting to keep the flexibility that Haugland praised while avoiding the monotony that troubled Ferrell. Whether such compromises succeed may depend less on the drawings themselves than on the economic forces that, as the history of the grid shows, have always shaped cities from beneath the surface."}],"groups":[{"type":"mhead","instructions":"Questions 14–20. Reading Passage 2 has seven paragraphs, A–G. Choose the correct heading for each paragraph from the list of headings below. There are more headings than paragraphs.","options":["i A layout suited to settling new land at speed","ii Ignoring the shape of the ground","iii How interchangeable plots encouraged speculation","iv An alternative design and what it came to represent","v Evidence that regular plans support future change","vi An old pattern viewed in a new light","vii An everyday feature with financial roots","viii Laws that prohibited curved streets","ix The technical problems of draining marshland"],"questions":[{"q":"Paragraph A","answer":"vii","explanation":"A: the grid seems ordinary but its history reveals as much about money and property as about design."},{"q":"Paragraph B","answer":"i","explanation":"B: the grid could be laid out quickly when new towns were founded rapidly."},{"q":"Paragraph C","answer":"iii","explanation":"C: interchangeable plots were traded by investors; speculation drove prices up and left empty lots."},{"q":"Paragraph D","answer":"ii","explanation":"D: the grid's indifference to landscape (hills, rivers and marshes). Heading ix picks up 'marshes' but the paragraph is not about drainage technology."},{"q":"Paragraph E","answer":"iv","explanation":"E: curved suburbs as an alternative that became a symbol of status. No laws are mentioned (viii)."},{"q":"Paragraph F","answer":"v","explanation":"F: Haugland's property records show grids allow land to be combined or divided as needs change."},{"q":"Paragraph G","answer":"vi","explanation":"G: planners have looked at the grid with fresh appreciation in recent decades."}]},{"type":"mfeat","instructions":"Questions 21–24. Look at the following statements and the list of people below. Match each statement with the correct person, A, B or C. You may use any letter more than once.","options":["A Teodora Blasko","B Joaquim Ferrell","C Lise Haugland"],"questions":[{"q":"Towns that developed slowly over time had a more distinctive character.","answer":"B","explanation":"D: Ferrell said older cities that grew gradually possessed a variety and a sense of place."},{"q":"Land was sometimes sold before any construction had begun.","answer":"A","explanation":"B: Blasko points out that lots were auctioned before a single building had been erected."},{"q":"The value of grid plots reacted faster to general economic conditions.","answer":"C","explanation":"F: Haugland found their prices responded more quickly to changes in the wider economy."},{"q":"Some towns lost much of their population within a few decades of being founded.","answer":"A","explanation":"C: Blasko notes several towns were half-abandoned within a generation."}]},{"type":"mcq2","instructions":"Questions 25–26. Choose TWO letters, A–E.","questions":[{"q":"Which TWO drawbacks of curved suburbs are mentioned in the passage?","options":["A They cost more to survey and to provide with services.","B They were noisier than the centre of the industrial city.","C They tended to lock in one pattern of land use for a long time.","D They were often built on land that flooded each spring.","E Wealthy buyers soon lost interest in them."],"answer":["A","C"],"explanation":"E: more expensive to survey and to supply with services; F: curved suburbs tended to fix a single pattern of use for a century or more. Flooding (D) is said of grid streets, and the suburbs offered escape from noise (B)."}]}]},{"title":"Is simplicity a sign of truth?","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"Scientists have long been advised to prefer simple explanations. When two theories account equally well for the same observations, the familiar rule says, choose the one that assumes less: fewer entities, fewer causes, fewer adjustable quantities. The principle is taught to students as though it were a settled part of scientific method, and it is regularly invoked in arguments about everything from the origins of a disease to the structure of the universe. I want to suggest that the principle, while useful, is far less secure than its popularity implies, and that we should be clearer about what it can and cannot do."},{"label":"B","text":"The difficulty begins with the word 'simple' itself. A theory may be simple in one respect and complicated in another. One explanation may involve a small number of underlying causes but require elaborate mathematics to connect them to anything observable; a rival may use only basic arithmetic yet assume a large number of separate mechanisms. Which is simpler? There is no neutral way to decide, because the answer depends on which feature one has chosen to count. Attempts to define simplicity formally, for instance by counting the number of symbols needed to write a theory down, run into the same problem, since the count changes with the notation chosen. Defenders of the principle sometimes respond that experienced researchers simply recognise simplicity when they see it. That may be true, but an intuition that cannot be stated precisely is a weak foundation for a rule of method."},{"label":"C","text":"A second problem concerns justification. Why should nature favour simple explanations? Some philosophers have argued that the history of science shows simple theories succeeding more often than complex ones. But this record is less impressive than it seems. The theories that survive are, almost by definition, the ones we have learned to regard as elegant, and many once-celebrated simple accounts were eventually replaced by messier successors that fitted the evidence better. To appeal to history is therefore to risk counting the winners and forgetting the losers. Moreover, the cases most often cited are drawn from physics, a field whose successes may say little about sciences that study living organisms or human societies."},{"label":"D","text":"There is, however, a more modest defence that I find persuasive. On this view, simplicity is not evidence that a theory is true but a practical guide to which theory we should test first. A simpler hypothesis typically makes sharper predictions and is easier to refute, so beginning with it allows errors to be exposed more quickly. Statisticians have made a related point: a model with too many adjustable quantities can be made to fit almost any set of data, including the random noise within it, and will then perform badly when applied to new cases. Preferring the leaner model protects against this danger, known as overfitting, not because nature is simple but because our data are limited. This is a claim about the reliability of our methods, not about the structure of reality."},{"label":"E","text":"This distinction matters more than it may appear. If simplicity is treated as a mark of truth, then a complex theory starts at a disadvantage regardless of the evidence, and researchers may dismiss findings that do not fit an elegant picture. Biology offers many examples of systems that turned out to be far more intricate than early models proposed; insisting on simplicity there would have delayed rather than advanced understanding. If, on the other hand, simplicity is understood as a strategy, it can be abandoned without embarrassment whenever the evidence demands it."},{"label":"F","text":"Critics of my position may reply that this is a distinction without a difference, since in practice scientists behave in the same way whichever interpretation they hold. I disagree. The interpretation shapes how confident we are entitled to be. A researcher who believes that the simplest theory is probably true will tend to present it as established; one who sees simplicity as a working rule will present it as the best current candidate and remain alert to its weaknesses. In public debates, where scientific claims influence policy, the second attitude is considerably more honest. Overstated certainty, once exposed, damages public trust in science far more than an honest admission that a question remains open."},{"label":"G","text":"None of this means that simplicity should be abandoned. A science without any preference for economy would drown in endless, untestable speculation, and the habit of asking whether an extra assumption is really needed remains one of the most valuable a researcher can acquire. My claim is narrower: that the principle earns its place through its usefulness in the process of inquiry, not through any guarantee about the world. Teaching it in that spirit would give students a more accurate picture of how science actually makes progress, and would help them to recognise the occasions on which the rule should give way."}],"groups":[{"type":"ynng","instructions":"Questions 27–32. Do the following statements agree with the claims of the writer in Reading Passage 3? Choose YES if the statement agrees with the claims of the writer, NO if the statement contradicts the claims of the writer, or NOT GIVEN if it is impossible to say what the writer thinks about this.","questions":[{"q":"The principle of simplicity is given more authority than it deserves.","options":["YES","NO","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"YES","explanation":"A: the principle is far less secure than its popularity implies."},{"q":"The ability of experienced researchers to recognise simplicity provides a sound basis for a rule of method.","options":["YES","NO","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"NO","explanation":"B: an intuition that cannot be stated precisely is a weak foundation for a rule of method."},{"q":"Arguments from the history of science tend to ignore simple theories that were later rejected.","options":["YES","NO","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"YES","explanation":"C: appealing to history risks counting the winners and forgetting the losers; many simple accounts were replaced."},{"q":"Statisticians are more willing than philosophers to accept the principle of simplicity.","options":["YES","NO","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"NOT GIVEN","explanation":"D mentions statisticians making a related point, but the writer never compares how willing statisticians and philosophers are to accept the principle."},{"q":"It makes little practical difference which interpretation of simplicity a scientist holds.","options":["YES","NO","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"NO","explanation":"F: critics say this, but the writer replies 'I disagree. The interpretation shapes how confident we are entitled to be.'"},{"q":"Complex systems are more common in biology than in any other science.","options":["YES","NO","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"NOT GIVEN","explanation":"E says biology offers many examples of intricate systems, but makes no comparison with other sciences."}]},{"type":"mcq","instructions":"Questions 33–36. Choose the correct letter, A, B, C or D.","questions":[{"q":"In paragraph B, the writer compares two rival explanations in order to show that","options":["A simple theories are usually expressed in mathematical terms.","B judgements about simplicity depend on what is being counted.","C complex theories are harder for students to learn.","D explanations involving many mechanisms are generally rejected."],"answer":"B","explanation":"B: there is no neutral way to decide, because the answer depends on which feature one has chosen to count."},{"q":"According to the 'more modest defence' in paragraph D, simpler hypotheses are useful because they","options":["A are more likely to be true.","B need less data to be collected.","C allow mistakes to be detected sooner.","D are preferred by most working scientists."],"answer":"C","explanation":"D: beginning with a simpler hypothesis allows errors to be exposed more quickly. Option A is the view the writer rejects."},{"q":"What does the writer suggest about biology in paragraph E?","options":["A Its early models were more accurate than later ones.","B A strict preference for simplicity could have held back its progress.","C It has been less influenced by the principle than other sciences.","D Its findings have often been dismissed by other scientists."],"answer":"B","explanation":"E: insisting on simplicity there would have delayed rather than advanced understanding."},{"q":"What is the writer's main purpose in paragraph G?","options":["A to propose that simplicity should no longer be taught","B to summarise the objections raised by critics","C to make clear how limited the writer's claim is","D to argue that simplicity guarantees scientific progress"],"answer":"C","explanation":"G: 'None of this means that simplicity should be abandoned... My claim is narrower.'"}]},{"type":"summ","instructions":"Questions 37–40. Complete the summary using the list of words, A–G, below.","outline":{"style":"summary","rows":[["Statisticians warn that a model with too many adjustable quantities can be made to match almost any set of data, even the random [1] that it contains. When such a model is applied to new cases, its results are likely to be [2]. Selecting a [3] model reduces this risk, which is known as overfitting. On this view, the reason for preferring it is not that nature is simple but that the data available to researchers are [4]."]]},"options":["A accurate","B limited","C noise","D complex","E poor","F leaner","G abundant"],"questions":[{"q":"Gap 1","answer":"C","explanation":"D: fit almost any set of data, including the random noise within it."},{"q":"Gap 2","answer":"E","explanation":"D: the model will then perform badly when applied to new cases."},{"q":"Gap 3","answer":"F","explanation":"D: preferring the leaner model protects against this danger."},{"q":"Gap 4","answer":"B","explanation":"D: not because nature is simple but because our data are limited."}]}]}]},{"id":"rt3","kind":"reading","title":"Reading test 3","passages":[{"title":"The rubbish heaps that rewrote a coastline's history","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"Along the low shores of the Calden estuary, walkers often pass grassy mounds without a second glance. To the untrained eye they look like natural dunes, but cut into one and a very different picture emerges: layer upon layer of discarded shells, fish bones, charcoal and broken tools. These mounds are middens, the accumulated refuse of communities who gathered food from the shore over many generations. For a long time they attracted little scholarly interest. Early collectors were drawn to burial sites and stone monuments, which promised impressive objects for museum display, and they regarded middens as little more than ancient rubbish tips. Farmers, meanwhile, sometimes dug the mounds away altogether and spread the crushed shell on their fields, so that a number of the largest examples disappeared before anyone had thought to study them."},{"label":"B","text":"That attitude began to change when archaeologists recognised that rubbish is often more honest than monuments. A monument records what a society wished to say about itself; a refuse heap records what people actually ate, made and threw away. Because a midden grows gradually, with each season's waste deposited on top of the last, it also functions as a kind of calendar. The lowest layers are the oldest, and by working upwards an excavator can follow changes in diet, technology and settlement across centuries. Shell, moreover, is chemically alkaline, and it neutralises the acids in the surrounding soil that would normally destroy bone. As a result, middens frequently preserve animal remains that have vanished from other sites in the same region."},{"label":"C","text":"Excavating a midden is a slow and painstaking process. Rather than removing the whole mound, which would destroy it, teams usually cut a narrow trench through one side to expose a vertical face, known as a section. The section is cleaned and photographed, and every visible layer is drawn to scale. Researchers then remove a column of material, block by block, from the top of the section down to the natural ground beneath. Each block is bagged separately and labelled with its depth, so that nothing can later be confused with material from a different level."},{"label":"D","text":"Much of the real work takes place away from the site. In the laboratory, each sample is washed through a series of sieves with progressively finer mesh. The largest fragments, such as shell and bone, are caught first, while the finest screens retain tiny seeds and the scales of small fish. Specialists then sort the material by hand and weigh each category. Weight rather than number is generally preferred, because a single crushed shell may break into dozens of pieces and give a misleading count. Finally, small pieces of charcoal from each layer are sent for radiocarbon dating, which allows the sequence to be fixed in time."},{"label":"E","text":"At Calden, this method produced an unexpected result. The lower layers of the largest midden were dominated by oysters, which require the warm, sheltered conditions of a muddy estuary. Higher up, oysters disappear almost entirely and are replaced by mussels and limpets, species that cling to rocks on exposed coasts. One explanation would be that the inhabitants had simply changed their tastes. However, the fish bones changed at the same time, from estuary species to fish of the open sea. The research team concluded that the estuary itself had altered: the shelter once provided by a long sand bar had been lost, probably during a period of storms, and the bay had become open to the ocean."},{"label":"F","text":"This interpretation has wider implications. Geologists had previously assumed that the Calden coastline had remained stable for several thousand years, partly because no written record described any dramatic change. The midden suggested otherwise, and later cores drilled from the seabed confirmed that a sand barrier had indeed been breached. In this sense, the people who left their refuse on the shore became, without ever intending it, the most reliable observers of their own environment. Their meals, preserved in the order in which they were eaten, recorded a transformation that no one at the time thought to write down."},{"label":"G","text":"Middens are now recognised as valuable archives, but they are also under threat. Many lie at the very edge of the shore, where rising sea levels and stronger waves are eroding them faster than archaeologists can record them. Some local groups have begun walking the coast after winter storms, photographing newly exposed sections and reporting them to regional heritage offices. Such volunteers cannot replace trained excavators, yet their observations have already identified several sites that would otherwise have been washed away unrecorded. For a type of site that was once dismissed as worthless, it is a striking reversal of fortune."}],"groups":[{"type":"tfng","instructions":"Questions 1–5. Do the following statements agree with the information given in Reading Passage 1? Choose TRUE if the statement agrees with the information, FALSE if the statement contradicts the information, or NOT GIVEN if there is no information on this.","questions":[{"q":"Early collectors favoured sites that were likely to provide items suitable for exhibition.","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"TRUE","explanation":"A: collectors were drawn to burial sites and monuments, which promised impressive objects for museum display."},{"q":"Bone survives well in middens because shell makes the soil more acidic.","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"FALSE","explanation":"B: shell is alkaline and neutralises the acids in the soil, so the reason given is the opposite."},{"q":"Each block of material removed from a section is marked with information about where it came from.","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"TRUE","explanation":"C: each block is bagged separately and labelled with its depth."},{"q":"Written accounts from the period describe severe storms on the Calden coast.","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"FALSE","explanation":"F: no written record described any dramatic change; the change was something no one at the time thought to write down."},{"q":"The volunteers who survey the coast after storms are trained by regional heritage offices.","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"NOT GIVEN","explanation":"G: volunteers report sites to heritage offices, but nothing is said about who trains them, or whether they are trained at all."}]},{"type":"diag","instructions":"Questions 6–9. Complete the flow-chart below. Choose ONE WORD ONLY from the passage for each answer.","outline":{"style":"flowchart","rows":[["A narrow [1] is cut into one side of the mound to expose a section"],["The section is cleaned, photographed and every layer is drawn to scale"],["Material is removed block by block; each block is bagged and labelled with its [2]"],["In the laboratory, samples are washed through [3] with increasingly fine mesh"],["Material is sorted and weighed; charcoal is sent for [4] dating"]]},"questions":[{"q":"Gap 1","answer":"trench","explanation":"C: teams usually cut a narrow trench through one side to expose a vertical face."},{"q":"Gap 2","answer":"depth","explanation":"C: each block is bagged separately and labelled with its depth."},{"q":"Gap 3","answer":"sieves","explanation":"D: each sample is washed through a series of sieves with progressively finer mesh."},{"q":"Gap 4","answer":"radiocarbon","explanation":"D: small pieces of charcoal from each layer are sent for radiocarbon dating."}]},{"type":"short","instructions":"Questions 10–13. Answer the questions below. Choose NO MORE THAN THREE WORDS from the passage for each answer.","questions":[{"q":"Which type of shellfish was most common in the oldest layers of the largest Calden midden?","answer":"oysters","explanation":"E: the lower layers of the largest midden were dominated by oysters. Mussels and limpets come from the higher, later layers."},{"q":"Which other evidence in the midden changed at the same time as the shellfish?","answer":"fish bones","alt":["the fish bones"],"explanation":"E: the fish bones changed at the same time, from estuary species to fish of the open sea."},{"q":"What natural feature had once protected the estuary from the open sea?","answer":"sand bar","alt":["a long sand bar","long sand bar","sand barrier","a sand barrier"],"explanation":"E: the shelter once provided by a long sand bar had been lost."},{"q":"What did researchers take from the seabed that supported the archaeologists' conclusion?","answer":"cores","explanation":"F: later cores drilled from the seabed confirmed that a sand barrier had been breached."}]}]},{"title":"Fair tests: how medical research guards against self-deception","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"When a patient takes a new remedy and then feels better, it is natural to conclude that the remedy worked. Yet such experiences, however convincing to the individual, are weak evidence. Many illnesses, from colds to back pain, clear up on their own, and a treatment taken during that time will receive credit it does not deserve. There is a subtler problem as well. People tend to seek help when their symptoms are at their worst, and since severe symptoms usually ease to some extent whatever is done, an improvement is likely to follow almost any intervention. The history of medicine contains many treatments, now abandoned, that were trusted for generations for exactly these reasons."},{"label":"B","text":"The basic solution is comparison. If one group of patients receives the new treatment and a similar group does not, any difference in outcome can more reasonably be attributed to the treatment. The difficulty lies in the word ‘similar’. If doctors are allowed to decide who receives the new therapy, they may, quite unconsciously, offer it to patients who seem stronger or more likely to cope, making the treatment appear more effective than it is. For this reason, modern trials allocate patients to groups at random. The epidemiologist Selma Draycott has stressed that randomisation is only effective if the sequence is concealed: if the staff recruiting patients can predict which group the next person will join, they may steer certain patients towards or away from the trial, and the advantage of chance is lost."},{"label":"C","text":"Even with random allocation, expectations can distort results. Patients who know they are receiving an active drug may report feeling better simply because they anticipate improvement, while those who know they are in the comparison group may be disappointed. Researchers who assess the outcomes are vulnerable in the same way, particularly when the measure involves judgement, such as rating pain or mood. The standard response is blinding: the comparison group receives a placebo that looks identical to the real treatment, and neither the patients nor the assessors are told who is receiving which. In a ‘double-blind’ trial of this kind, expectations should affect both groups equally. Blinding is not always possible, however. It is hard to disguise whether someone has had surgery or joined an exercise programme."},{"label":"D","text":"Some questions cannot be answered by trials at all. It would be impractical, and in many cases unethical, to assign thousands of people to a particular diet for thirty years, or to ask them to take up a harmful habit. Here researchers rely on observational studies, which follow people as they live their ordinary lives and look for links between behaviour and health. The weakness of this approach is confounding: people who differ in one habit often differ in many others. Those who eat a lot of vegetables, for instance, may also exercise more and smoke less. Statistical methods can adjust for factors that have been measured, but not for those nobody thought to record. Nonetheless, the medical statistician Paulo Venn argues that dismissing observational research would be a serious mistake, since much of what is known about the causes of disease could never have been discovered by experiment."},{"label":"E","text":"The size of a study also matters. In a small trial, a handful of unusual patients can produce a dramatic difference between groups purely by chance. Such results attract attention, but they are often not repeated when larger studies are carried out. Anika Rhodell, who has compared early and later trials of the same treatments, describes a recurring pattern in which the benefits reported in the first small studies shrink, sometimes to nothing, as evidence accumulates. Her conclusion is not that small studies are worthless, but that their findings should be treated as provisional until they have been confirmed."},{"label":"F","text":"A further distortion arises after the research is complete. Studies with positive results are more likely to be submitted to journals and more likely to be published, while those that find no effect may simply be filed away. Anyone reviewing the published literature will therefore see an exaggerated picture. A related temptation is for researchers to change their main outcome after seeing the data, highlighting whichever measure happened to show a benefit. To counter both problems, many funders and journals now require trials to be registered before they begin, with the planned outcomes stated publicly in advance."},{"label":"G","text":"Finally, a trial can be rigorous and yet of limited use. To obtain clear results, researchers often exclude elderly people, pregnant women and patients with several conditions at once, even though these are precisely the people who will receive the treatment in practice. So-called pragmatic trials attempt to close this gap by testing treatments in ordinary clinics with typical patients. Draycott welcomes the trend but warns that there is a price: the more a trial resembles real life, the more variable its results tend to be, and the harder it becomes to draw firm conclusions."}],"groups":[{"type":"mhead","instructions":"Questions 14–20. Reading Passage 2 has seven paragraphs, A–G. Choose the correct heading for each paragraph from the list of headings below. There are more headings than paragraphs.","options":["i The unreliability of early, limited studies","ii Letting chance decide who receives treatment","iii The rising cost of large medical trials","iv Bringing trials closer to everyday practice","v Why feeling better after treatment proves little","vi Preventing beliefs from influencing outcomes","vii How patients are persuaded to volunteer","viii Hidden results and shifting goals","ix Research that observes rather than intervenes"],"questions":[{"q":"Paragraph A","answer":"v","explanation":"A: improvement after a remedy is weak evidence because illnesses clear up on their own and symptoms ease from their worst point."},{"q":"Paragraph B","answer":"ii","explanation":"B: modern trials allocate patients to groups at random so that doctors' choices do not bias the comparison."},{"q":"Paragraph C","answer":"vi","explanation":"C: expectations of patients and assessors can distort results, so trials use placebos and blinding."},{"q":"Paragraph D","answer":"ix","explanation":"D: observational studies follow people in their ordinary lives instead of assigning treatments."},{"q":"Paragraph E","answer":"i","explanation":"E: small trials can produce dramatic results by chance, and early benefits often shrink later."},{"q":"Paragraph F","answer":"viii","explanation":"F: negative studies are filed away (hidden results) and researchers may change their main outcome (shifting goals). Cost (iii) is never discussed."},{"q":"Paragraph G","answer":"iv","explanation":"G: pragmatic trials test treatments in ordinary clinics with typical patients. Recruitment of volunteers (vii) is not discussed."}]},{"type":"mfeat","instructions":"Questions 21–24. Look at the following statements and the list of researchers below. Match each statement with the correct researcher, A, B or C. You may use any letter more than once.","options":["A Selma Draycott","B Paulo Venn","C Anika Rhodell"],"questions":[{"q":"The apparent advantages of a treatment often become smaller as more research is carried out.","answer":"C","explanation":"E: Rhodell describes benefits in the first small studies that shrink as evidence accumulates."},{"q":"Studies that do not assign treatments have revealed things that experiments never could.","answer":"B","explanation":"D: Venn argues that much of what is known about the causes of disease could never have been discovered by experiment."},{"q":"Random allocation loses its value if recruiters can tell in advance where a patient will be placed.","answer":"A","explanation":"B: Draycott stresses that the sequence must be concealed; if staff can predict the next group, the advantage of chance is lost."},{"q":"Making a study more realistic tends to make its findings less clear-cut.","answer":"A","explanation":"G: Draycott warns that the more a trial resembles real life, the harder it becomes to draw firm conclusions."}]},{"type":"mcq2","instructions":"Questions 25–26. Choose TWO letters, A–E.","questions":[{"q":"Which TWO reasons does the writer give to explain why a patient may feel better after taking a treatment that does not work?","options":["A Many conditions disappear without any treatment.","B Doctors often prescribe more of a drug than is needed.","C Patients usually consult a doctor when their symptoms are most severe.","D Patients tend to exaggerate their recovery to please their doctor.","E Older treatments were tested on too few patients."],"answer":["A","C"],"explanation":"A: many illnesses clear up on their own, and people seek help when symptoms are at their worst, so some improvement follows almost any intervention. B, D and E are not given as reasons."}]}]},{"title":"Automation and the news: a debate worth having properly","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"Every new communication technology seems to arrive accompanied by two predictions. The first is that it will spread knowledge to everyone and sweep away the old gatekeepers; the second is that it will corrupt public taste and destroy serious culture. Both were made about the printing press, about radio and about television, and both are now being made about the automation of news. In my view, neither prediction is very useful. They share a tendency to treat technology as a force that acts on society from outside, when in reality it is shaped at every stage by commercial decisions and by the habits of the people who use it."},{"label":"B","text":"Consider first the software that now writes news reports. A number of media organisations already use programs that turn structured data, such as company earnings, election counts or football results, into short articles within seconds of the figures being released. Critics describe this as the beginning of the end for human journalism. I find the alarm exaggerated. The stories produced in this way are precisely those that reporters have always found tedious: formulaic summaries that required little judgement and offered little satisfaction. If a machine can produce a reliable account of a regional football match, a journalist is released to investigate why the local stadium was sold at a fraction of its value."},{"label":"C","text":"That, at least, is the optimistic version. The less comfortable possibility is that managers will treat automation not as a way to redirect skilled staff but as a reason to employ fewer of them. Newspapers have been losing advertising revenue for many years, and an owner under financial pressure may see little reason to fund slow, expensive investigations when cheap automated content can fill the pages. Here the critics have a point, but it is important to locate the problem correctly. The threat to investigative reporting comes from the business model, not from the software, and blaming the machine distracts attention from the choices of those who own it."},{"label":"D","text":"A more serious concern, I would argue, lies elsewhere: in the systems that decide which stories people see. Most readers now encounter news not by opening a newspaper but through feeds arranged by recommendation algorithms. These systems are typically designed to maximise engagement, measured by clicks, shares and time spent on a page. Material that provokes anger or fear tends to perform well by these measures, while careful explanation performs poorly. Over time, an algorithm can learn to reward the very content that a responsible editor would have placed at the back of the paper, or left out altogether."},{"label":"E","text":"Defenders of recommendation systems reply that they merely give people what they want, and that the old editors were hardly neutral. The second half of this argument is fair; newspapers have always had political loyalties and commercial interests. Yet a traditional newspaper's biases were at least visible. Readers knew which paper they were holding and could compare it with a rival. The ranking decisions of an algorithm, by contrast, are hidden, constantly changing and different for every user, so that no two people can easily compare what they have been shown. It is this invisibility, rather than bias as such, that troubles me."},{"label":"F","text":"Some commentators have proposed that governments should require platforms to show feeds in time order by default, or to promote news judged to be in the public interest. Whether such rules would survive a legal challenge is uncertain, and I am wary of handing the state a role in deciding what counts as important news. A more modest measure seems to me both practical and overdue: platforms should be obliged to explain, in plain language, the main factors that determine what each user sees, and to allow independent researchers to examine how their systems behave. Transparency will not solve every problem, but it would allow public debate to rest on evidence rather than suspicion."},{"label":"G","text":"Nor should readers be regarded as passive victims. People commonly say that they value accurate, in-depth reporting, even when their clicking behaviour suggests otherwise, and that gap between stated preference and habit is something that education can address. To assume that audiences are incapable of choosing well is not only condescending; it also excuses the companies concerned from any duty to offer them better choices. Schools could do far more to teach young people how feeds are assembled and how to seek out sources deliberately. Automation is not destiny. The technologies that write and distribute news will reflect the priorities of the people who build, own and use them, and those priorities remain open to argument. That is precisely why the debate deserves to be conducted with more care than the familiar choice between celebration and despair."}],"groups":[{"type":"ynng","instructions":"Questions 27–31. Do the following statements agree with the claims of the writer in Reading Passage 3? Choose YES if the statement agrees with the claims of the writer, NO if the statement contradicts the claims of the writer, or NOT GIVEN if it is impossible to say what the writer thinks about this.","questions":[{"q":"The familiar optimistic and pessimistic forecasts about new media are of little value.","options":["YES","NO","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"YES","explanation":"A: “In my view, neither prediction is very useful.”"},{"q":"Software that writes news reports signals the end of human journalism.","options":["YES","NO","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"NO","explanation":"B: critics say this, but the writer says “I find the alarm exaggerated.”"},{"q":"Financial pressures, rather than the technology itself, are the main danger to investigative journalism.","options":["YES","NO","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"YES","explanation":"C: the threat comes from the business model, not from the software."},{"q":"Traditional newspaper editors made their choices without political bias.","options":["YES","NO","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"NO","explanation":"E: the writer agrees that the old editors were hardly neutral and that newspapers always had political loyalties."},{"q":"Showing feeds in time order would reduce the spread of content that provokes anger.","options":["YES","NO","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"NOT GIVEN","explanation":"F: the writer mentions the proposal and doubts about state involvement, but never says whether time-ordered feeds would reduce angry content."}]},{"type":"minfo","instructions":"Questions 32–35. Reading Passage 3 has seven paragraphs, A–G. Which paragraph contains the following information? You may use any letter more than once.","options":["A","B","C","D","E","F","G"],"questions":[{"q":"a reference to a difference between what people claim to want and what they actually do","answer":"G","explanation":"G: people say they value in-depth reporting even when their clicking behaviour suggests otherwise."},{"q":"an example of a story a reporter might investigate once freed from routine tasks","answer":"B","explanation":"B: a journalist is released to investigate why the local stadium was sold at a fraction of its value."},{"q":"a reason why the choices of an algorithm are harder to scrutinise than those of a newspaper","answer":"E","explanation":"E: an algorithm's ranking decisions are hidden, constantly changing and different for every user."},{"q":"a mention of earlier inventions that provoked similar hopes and fears","answer":"A","explanation":"A: both predictions were made about the printing press, radio and television."}]},{"type":"summ","instructions":"Questions 36–40. Complete the summary using the list of words, A–I, below.","outline":{"style":"summary","rows":[["Recommendation algorithms are generally built to increase [1], so material that causes [2] or fear tends to be rewarded, while careful [3] does badly. Although some people want governments to regulate news feeds, the writer is reluctant to give the [4] any say over which news matters. Instead, platforms should have to describe the main [5] that decide what each user sees, and allow independent study of their systems."]]},"options":["A engagement","B anger","C explanation","D state","E factors","F advertising","G accuracy","H researchers","I profits"],"questions":[{"q":"Gap 1","answer":"A","explanation":"D: these systems are typically designed to maximise engagement. Advertising (F) is mentioned only in paragraph C."},{"q":"Gap 2","answer":"B","explanation":"D: material that provokes anger or fear tends to perform well."},{"q":"Gap 3","answer":"C","explanation":"D: careful explanation performs poorly."},{"q":"Gap 4","answer":"D","explanation":"F: the writer is wary of handing the state a role in deciding what counts as important news. Researchers (H) are people the writer wants to give access to, not keep out."},{"q":"Gap 5","answer":"E","explanation":"F: platforms should explain the main factors that determine what each user sees."}]}]}]},{"id":"lt1","kind":"listening","title":"Listening test 1","sections":[{"title":"Booking a house move","context":"Section 1: A woman phones a removals company to ask for a quote for moving house.","lines":[{"speaker":"Agent","text":"Good morning, Swift and Steady Removals, this is Gareth speaking. How can I help you?"},{"speaker":"Mrs Hartigan","text":"Oh, hello. I'm moving house next month and I'd like to get a quote, please."},{"speaker":"Agent","text":"Certainly. I'll just take a few details first, and then I can give you a rough estimate over the phone. Could I have your surname?"},{"speaker":"Mrs Hartigan","text":"Yes, it's Hartigan. That's H, A, R, T, I, G, A, N."},{"speaker":"Agent","text":"H, A, R, T, I, G, A, N. And your first name?"},{"speaker":"Mrs Hartigan","text":"Jean."},{"speaker":"Agent","text":"Thank you, Mrs Hartigan. And where are you moving from?"},{"speaker":"Mrs Hartigan","text":"My current address is twenty-three Moss Lane, in Brackley. Oh, goodness, no, what am I saying? Twenty-three was my last house. It's thirty-two Moss Lane."},{"speaker":"Agent","text":"Thirty-two Moss Lane, Brackley. Don't worry, people do that all the time. And is that a house or a flat?"},{"speaker":"Mrs Hartigan","text":"It's a house, a small one with two bedrooms. But where I'm going is a flat."},{"speaker":"Agent","text":"Right. Which floor is the new flat on? That makes quite a difference for us."},{"speaker":"Mrs Hartigan","text":"Well, when I first looked round the building they showed me one on the ground floor, but it was very dark, so I've taken one on the third floor instead. There is a lift, though."},{"speaker":"Agent","text":"That's good news for my team. A lift saves a lot of time. Now, have you got a date in mind?"},{"speaker":"Mrs Hartigan","text":"I was hoping for Saturday the fourteenth of June."},{"speaker":"Agent","text":"Let me check. Hmm, I'm afraid the fourteenth is already fully booked. We do have the following Saturday, the twenty-first."},{"speaker":"Mrs Hartigan","text":"The twenty-first. Yes, that should be fine, actually. The flat is mine from the twelfth, so there's no rush. Let's say the twenty-first."},{"speaker":"Agent","text":"Lovely, I'll put that in the diary. Now, are there any items that need special care? Pianos, large antiques, anything like that?"},{"speaker":"Mrs Hartigan","text":"Well, I used to have a piano, but I sold it last year because nobody ever played it. The thing I'm really worried about is a mirror. It's very old, it belonged to my grandmother, and the glass is quite thin."},{"speaker":"Agent","text":"We'll put that in a padded wooden crate, so it'll be perfectly safe. What about the rest of the packing? We can pack everything for you, or just certain rooms."},{"speaker":"Mrs Hartigan","text":"I'm happy to do most of it myself. I've got plenty of time now that I've retired. But the kitchen is full of glasses and plates, and I'd rather you did that. So just the kitchen, please."},{"speaker":"Agent","text":"Fine, packing for the kitchen only. Can I ask what's prompting the move? Sometimes it helps us plan."},{"speaker":"Mrs Hartigan","text":"Everyone assumes it's about money, because the house costs a fortune to heat, and that's true enough. But really it's that my daughter and the grandchildren live in Ashford now, and I'd like to be near them. The flat is only ten minutes' walk from their house."},{"speaker":"Agent","text":"That sounds lovely. And may I ask how you heard about us?"},{"speaker":"Mrs Hartigan","text":"I saw one of your vans on the motorway a while ago, but I didn't write down the number. Then a neighbour of mine, Mr Ellis, told me you'd moved him last spring and were very careful, so I looked you up online and rang."},{"speaker":"Agent","text":"I'm very glad to hear that. Now, parking. Is there room for a large lorry outside the new building?"},{"speaker":"Mrs Hartigan","text":"The street at the front is very narrow, and there are double yellow lines all along it. I did wonder about the public car park round the corner, but the building manager told me there's a service entrance at the back, with a loading bay."},{"speaker":"Agent","text":"Perfect, we'll use the loading bay at the back, then. And finally, insurance. Basic cover is included in our price, and it covers up to five thousand pounds. You can upgrade to full cover for an extra forty pounds."},{"speaker":"Mrs Hartigan","text":"My home insurance might cover the move, but I'm honestly not sure it does. And with the mirror, I'd feel happier paying the extra. I'll take the full cover."},{"speaker":"Agent","text":"Very sensible. I'll email you a written quote this afternoon, and you can confirm the booking whenever you're ready."},{"speaker":"Mrs Hartigan","text":"Wonderful. Thank you so much, Gareth."}],"groups":[{"type":"lform","instructions":"Questions 1–6. Complete the form below. Write ONE WORD AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer.","outline":{"style":"form","rows":[["Customer:","Mrs Jean [1]"],["Current address:","[2] Moss Lane, Brackley (two-bedroom house)"],["New home:","flat on the [3] floor (lift available)"],["Moving date:","Saturday [4] June"],["Item needing special care:","an old [5] (to go in a padded crate)"],["Packing service required for:","the [6] only"]]},"questions":[{"q":"Gap 1","answer":"Hartigan","explanation":"She spells it: H, A, R, T, I, G, A, N."},{"q":"Gap 2","answer":"32","alt":["thirty-two","thirty two"],"explanation":"She first says twenty-three, then corrects herself: twenty-three was her last house; it's thirty-two Moss Lane."},{"q":"Gap 3","answer":"third","alt":["3rd"],"explanation":"She was shown a ground-floor flat but took one on the third floor instead."},{"q":"Gap 4","answer":"21","alt":["twenty-first","21st","twenty first"],"explanation":"The fourteenth is fully booked, so they agree on the twenty-first."},{"q":"Gap 5","answer":"mirror","explanation":"The piano was sold; the item she is worried about is her grandmother's old mirror."},{"q":"Gap 6","answer":"kitchen","explanation":"She will pack most things herself: 'So just the kitchen, please.'"}]},{"type":"lmcq","instructions":"Questions 7–10. Choose the correct letter, A, B or C.","questions":[{"q":"What is the main reason Mrs Hartigan is moving?","options":["A to reduce her heating costs","B to live closer to her family","C to be nearer to her workplace"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Everyone assumes it is about money, but 'really' it is to be near her daughter and grandchildren. She has retired, so C is wrong."},{"q":"How did Mrs Hartigan find out about the company?","options":["A She noted the number on one of its vans.","B A neighbour recommended it.","C She saw an advertisement online."],"answer":"B","explanation":"She saw a van but did not write down the number; her neighbour Mr Ellis said they were very careful, and only then did she look them up online."},{"q":"Where will the removal lorry park at the new building?","options":["A in the street at the front","B in a public car park nearby","C in a loading bay behind the building"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The front street is narrow with double yellow lines; she wondered about the car park, but the manager said there is a loading bay at the back."},{"q":"What does Mrs Hartigan decide to do about insurance?","options":["A rely on the basic cover","B pay for full cover","C use her own home insurance"],"answer":"B","explanation":"She is not sure her home insurance covers the move, so she takes the full cover for an extra forty pounds."}]}]},{"title":"The new Kingsford Library","context":"Section 2: A library manager talks to local residents at the reopening of their town library.","lines":[{"speaker":"Library manager","text":"Good morning, everyone, and welcome to the newly reopened Kingsford Library. My name's Alison Brady, and I'm the manager here. I know many of you have been waiting a long time for this day, so thank you all for your patience during eighteen months of building work. I'll start by explaining where everything is, and then I'll tell you about some of our new services."},{"speaker":"Library manager","text":"We're standing just inside the main entrance, which is at the bottom of the plan you were given at the door. Straight ahead of you, in the very centre of the building, is the reception desk, where you can borrow and return books, pay for printing, and ask the staff for help."},{"speaker":"Library manager","text":"If you've come with a coffee in mind, the café is the first room you'll reach. In the old library it was upstairs, which a lot of people found inconvenient, but now it's right here, immediately to your left as you come in through the entrance. It has big windows onto the street, so it's lovely and bright."},{"speaker":"Library manager","text":"On the other side of the entrance, so to your right, is the exhibition space. At the moment it's showing paintings by pupils from the local secondary school, and the displays will change every two months, so do keep coming back."},{"speaker":"Library manager","text":"Now, for families. The original plans put the children's area next to the café, but we changed our minds, and it's now at the back of the building, in the far left-hand corner. We deliberately placed it as far as possible from the study rooms, so that nobody is disturbed by the noise. And speaking of the study rooms, you'll find those on the right-hand side of the building, directly to the right of the reception desk. There are four of them, and they can be booked online for up to three hours at a time."},{"speaker":"Library manager","text":"The computer suite is right behind the reception desk, against the back wall, between the children's area and the local history room. The local history room itself is in the back right-hand corner. It holds maps, photographs and newspapers going back two hundred years. Please note that the area to the left of the reception desk is still closed. It's going to be a quiet reading room, but I'm afraid the furniture hasn't arrived yet."},{"speaker":"Library manager","text":"Right, let me tell you about some of the changes to our services. Membership is still free for anyone who lives or works in the district, as it always has been. What's new is that you no longer have to come in with proof of address. You can now join online, and it only takes a few minutes. Children under twelve still need an adult to sign the form, just as before."},{"speaker":"Library manager","text":"Opening hours. A lot of you asked us to open on Sundays, and we did look into it very seriously, but sadly we couldn't find the funding. Instead, we've extended our hours on Thursdays, when we'll now be open until eight in the evening. Weekday mornings stay the same, so we open at nine o'clock."},{"speaker":"Library manager","text":"We also have a full programme of events. Our reading group meets once a month, on the first Tuesday. The homework club for secondary pupils runs every Wednesday afternoon, every single week during term time. And we're hoping to arrange talks by local authors, probably two or three times a year."},{"speaker":"Library manager","text":"Finally, volunteers. We've got plenty of people helping out on the reception desk, thank you, and the local history society is already cataloguing the old photographs. Where we really need help is with our home delivery service, taking books to people who can't get to the library themselves. If you have a car and could spare an hour or two a week, please come and have a word with me at the end."}],"groups":[{"type":"lmap","instructions":"Questions 11–16. Label the plan of Kingsford Library. Choose the correct letter, A–G. You are at the main entrance.","map":{"cols":3,"rows":3,"cells":[{"r":0,"c":0,"t":"A"},{"r":0,"c":1,"t":"B"},{"r":0,"c":2,"t":"C"},{"r":1,"c":0,"t":"D"},{"r":1,"c":1,"t":"Reception desk"},{"r":1,"c":2,"t":"E"},{"r":2,"c":0,"t":"F"},{"r":2,"c":1,"t":"Main entrance"},{"r":2,"c":2,"t":"G"}]},"options":["A","B","C","D","E","F","G"],"questions":[{"q":"Café","answer":"F","explanation":"It used to be upstairs; now it is immediately to your left as you come in."},{"q":"Exhibition space","answer":"G","explanation":"On the other side of the entrance, to your right."},{"q":"Children's area","answer":"A","explanation":"The plans put it next to the café, but it is now at the back, in the far left-hand corner."},{"q":"Study rooms","answer":"E","explanation":"On the right-hand side, directly to the right of the reception desk."},{"q":"Computer suite","answer":"B","explanation":"Right behind the reception desk, against the back wall, between the children's area and the local history room."},{"q":"Local history room","answer":"C","explanation":"In the back right-hand corner. D, to the left of reception, is the future quiet reading room."}]},{"type":"lmcq","instructions":"Questions 17–20. Choose the correct letter, A, B or C.","questions":[{"q":"What is new about library membership?","options":["A It is now free for local residents.","B People can now join online.","C Children no longer need an adult's signature."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Membership was always free, and children under twelve still need an adult to sign; the new thing is joining online without proof of address."},{"q":"How have the opening hours changed?","options":["A The library opens earlier on weekdays.","B The library stays open later on one evening.","C The library now opens on Sundays."],"answer":"B","explanation":"There was no funding for Sundays and weekday mornings are unchanged; it is now open until eight on Thursdays."},{"q":"Which event takes place every week?","options":["A a reading group","B a homework club","C a talk by a local author"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The reading group is monthly and author talks two or three times a year; the homework club runs every Wednesday."},{"q":"Volunteers are most needed to","options":["A help at the reception desk.","B take books to people at home.","C sort the local history collection."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Reception has plenty of helpers and the history society is cataloguing photographs; the real need is the home delivery service."}]}]},{"title":"A project on canteen food waste","context":"Section 3: Two students, Leah and Marcus, discuss their research project on food waste in the university canteen.","lines":[{"speaker":"Marcus","text":"Hi, Leah. Have you got a few minutes to go over the food waste project before we see Dr Okafor on Friday?"},{"speaker":"Leah","text":"Sure. I've brought the notes from last week. Shall we start with how we collected the data, since that's the first part of the report?"},{"speaker":"Marcus","text":"Yes, good idea. Well, the main method was weighing what was left on the plates when the trays came back to the kitchen. We did that for two weeks, at lunchtime only."},{"speaker":"Leah","text":"And we also took photographs of a sample of plates, so we could see exactly which foods were being left. I think that worked really well, actually."},{"speaker":"Marcus","text":"It did. We'd planned to interview the kitchen staff as well, but in the end the manager said they were far too busy, so that never happened."},{"speaker":"Leah","text":"Right. And should we mention the kitchen bins? We talked about going through them."},{"speaker":"Marcus","text":"We only talked about it. We never actually did it, because of the hygiene rules. And the questionnaire was really part of the second stage, about solutions, so I don't think we should describe it as a way of measuring waste."},{"speaker":"Leah","text":"Agreed. So it's the weighing and the photographs for the measurement section. Now, the results. What surprised me most was how much bread was thrown away. I'd assumed it would be vegetables."},{"speaker":"Marcus","text":"Me too. Though the vegetables were roughly what we'd predicted, to be honest, quite high but not shocking. The other thing I really didn't expect was the difference between days. Friday was far worse than any other day of the week."},{"speaker":"Leah","text":"Yes, that was a real shock. Whereas the fact that larger portions led to more waste, well, that's fairly obvious, isn't it?"},{"speaker":"Marcus","text":"Exactly. And it was hardly news that hot meals were wasted less than salads. The canteen manager told us that on the very first day."},{"speaker":"Leah","text":"OK. So then we need to go through the possible solutions and say something about each one. Let's start with smaller plates."},{"speaker":"Marcus","text":"The canteen manager was completely against that. She said students would just come back for second helpings and the queues would get even longer."},{"speaker":"Leah","text":"Fine, so we'll report that she opposed it. What about tray-free days?"},{"speaker":"Marcus","text":"That's the one with real evidence behind it. Westbridge University introduced tray-free days three years ago, and their waste fell noticeably, so we can say it's already been shown to work elsewhere."},{"speaker":"Leah","text":"Good. Then pay-by-weight pricing. I think that was the one the students liked most in the questionnaire, wasn't it?"},{"speaker":"Marcus","text":"No, actually, look at the results again. It was one of the least popular. But I don't think that's the main issue anyway. Each set of scales costs several thousand pounds, and the canteen would need six of them."},{"speaker":"Leah","text":"Oh, you're right. So the cost is really what counts against it. And selling leftovers at a discount at the end of the day?"},{"speaker":"Marcus","text":"Now that was the students' favourite. Nearly three-quarters of the people who answered the questionnaire said they'd buy them."},{"speaker":"Leah","text":"Great. And composting the waste on campus? Isn't that something Westbridge does as well?"},{"speaker":"Marcus","text":"No, that was a different university, and I think they stopped. Anyway, the manager didn't object to composting at all, but the equipment would cost a fortune, and there's no budget for it this year."},{"speaker":"Leah","text":"So the same problem as the scales. And the last one was awareness posters in the canteen."},{"speaker":"Marcus","text":"The manager refused point blank. She said posters on every wall make the place look untidy, and nobody reads them anyway."},{"speaker":"Leah","text":"Well, that's clear enough. I'll write up the solutions section tonight if you can do the charts for the results."},{"speaker":"Marcus","text":"Deal. I'll send them to you by Wednesday, so we've got time to check everything before Friday."}],"groups":[{"type":"lmcq2","instructions":"Questions 21–24. Choose TWO letters, A–E, for each question.","questions":[{"q":"Questions 21–22: Which TWO methods did the students use to measure food waste?","options":["A weighing food left on plates","B photographing plates","C interviewing kitchen staff","D giving students a questionnaire","E checking the kitchen bins"],"answer":["A","B"],"explanation":"They weighed leftovers and photographed plates. Staff interviews never happened, the bins were only discussed, and the questionnaire was about solutions."},{"q":"Questions 23–24: Which TWO findings surprised the students?","options":["A the amount of bread wasted","B the amount of vegetables wasted","C the level of waste on one particular day","D the link between portion size and waste","E the difference between hot meals and salads"],"answer":["A","C"],"explanation":"Bread and the Friday figures surprised them; vegetables were as predicted, portion size was 'fairly obvious' and the hot meals point was 'hardly news'."}]},{"type":"lmatch","instructions":"Questions 25–30. What do the students say about each possible solution? Choose the correct letter, A–D. You may use any letter more than once.","options":["A It has already worked at another university.","B It would be too expensive.","C It was popular in the student questionnaire.","D The canteen manager is against it."],"questions":[{"q":"smaller plates","answer":"D","explanation":"The canteen manager was completely against it because of second helpings and queues."},{"q":"tray-free days","answer":"A","explanation":"Westbridge University introduced them and their waste fell."},{"q":"pay-by-weight pricing","answer":"B","explanation":"Leah thinks it was popular, but Marcus corrects her: it was one of the least popular, and the scales cost several thousand pounds each."},{"q":"discounted leftovers","answer":"C","explanation":"The students' favourite: nearly three-quarters said they would buy them."},{"q":"composting on campus","answer":"B","explanation":"It was not Westbridge, and the manager did not object; the equipment would cost a fortune."},{"q":"awareness posters","answer":"D","explanation":"The manager refused point blank."}]}]},{"title":"The natural ice trade","context":"Section 4: Part of a history lecture on how ice was harvested and stored before refrigeration.","lines":[{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"Good afternoon. In this lecture I'm going to look at something that most of us never think about: how people kept food and drink cold before the invention of the electric refrigerator. The answer, for several centuries, was natural ice, cut from frozen lakes in winter and stored until the summer."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"For most of its history, ice was a luxury. Ordinary families simply did without it, and it was enjoyed mainly by the wealthy, who could afford to build their own storage on their country estates. These structures were known as ice houses, and their design was surprisingly sophisticated."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"Most ice houses were built partly underground, where the temperature stays fairly constant. Builders were careful about which way the building faced. The entrance usually faced north, so that the doorway received as little direct sunlight as possible, and trees were often planted around it for extra shade."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"Insulation was the key to the whole design. The walls were usually built in two layers of brick, and the gap between them was filled with charcoal, which conducts heat very poorly. Some owners experimented with sand instead, but it was found to hold the damp. And of course, some of the ice always melted, so at the bottom of the pit there was a drain to carry away the meltwater. Standing water was the great enemy, because it melted the ice around it much faster."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"In the nineteenth century, ice stopped being a private luxury and became a business. You might expect that the main customers were households, but in fact the biggest buyers were breweries, which needed to keep beer cool while it was being made and stored. Fishmongers and hospitals came next. Huge quantities of ice were shipped across oceans, and to stop it melting on the voyage, it was packed tightly in sawdust, a cheap waste product from the timber mills."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"So how was the ice actually harvested? Let me take you through the process. First of all, the workers had to wait until the ice on the lake was thick enough, usually about thirty centimetres. Any snow on the surface had to be removed first, because it acted like a blanket and slowed down freezing. This was done with large scrapers, which were horse-drawn, as no person could pull them across such a large area."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"Next, the surface was marked out in a grid, and deep grooves were cut along the lines using an ice plough. Early on, workers used hand saws for this, but the plough was much faster and produced blocks of exactly the same size, which made them easier to stack."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"The blocks were then broken off and pushed along an open channel of water that had been cut through the ice, all the way to the shore. Floating them was far easier than lifting them. Finally, at the ice house, the blocks were stacked in layers, and each layer was separated by straw, so that the blocks didn't freeze together into one solid mass. On some estates sawdust was used here as well, but straw was more common because it was grown on the estate itself."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"The natural ice trade lasted until the early twentieth century. Its decline is usually explained by the arrival of refrigeration machines, but there was another important factor. As cities grew, the lakes and rivers near them became polluted, and doctors began to warn that natural ice could spread disease. Customers turned to factory-made ice, and the old ice houses were abandoned. Many of them survive today, and next week we'll visit one."}],"groups":[{"type":"lnote","instructions":"Questions 31–36. Complete the notes below. Write ONE WORD ONLY for each answer.","outline":{"style":"notes","rows":[["The natural ice trade"],["• Before refrigeration, ice was used mainly by the [1]"],["Design of ice houses"],["• usually partly underground; the entrance faced [2]"],["• walls: two layers of brick with [3] between them as insulation"],["• a drain at the bottom removed [4]"],["The commercial trade (19th century)"],["• largest customers were [5], not households"],["• ice was packed in [6] for long sea journeys"]]},"questions":[{"q":"Gap 1","answer":"wealthy","alt":["the wealthy"],"explanation":"Ordinary families did without it; it was enjoyed mainly by the wealthy."},{"q":"Gap 2","answer":"north","explanation":"The entrance usually faced north, to receive as little sunlight as possible."},{"q":"Gap 3","answer":"charcoal","explanation":"The gap was filled with charcoal; sand was tried but held the damp."},{"q":"Gap 4","answer":"meltwater","alt":["water"],"explanation":"A drain at the bottom carried away the meltwater."},{"q":"Gap 5","answer":"breweries","explanation":"You might expect households, but the biggest buyers were breweries."},{"q":"Gap 6","answer":"sawdust","explanation":"It was packed tightly in sawdust, a waste product from timber mills."}]},{"type":"lflow","instructions":"Questions 37–40. Complete the flow-chart below. Write ONE WORD ONLY for each answer.","outline":{"style":"flowchart","rows":[["Wait until the lake ice is about 30 cm thick"],["Remove snow from the surface with [1]-drawn scrapers"],["Cut grooves in a grid using an ice [2]"],["Push the blocks along an open [3] of water to the shore"],["Stack the blocks in the ice house with [4] between the layers"]]},"questions":[{"q":"Gap 1","answer":"horse","explanation":"The scrapers were horse-drawn, as no person could pull them."},{"q":"Gap 2","answer":"plough","alt":["plow"],"explanation":"Grooves were cut using an ice plough; hand saws were used only early on."},{"q":"Gap 3","answer":"channel","explanation":"Blocks were pushed along an open channel of water cut through the ice."},{"q":"Gap 4","answer":"straw","explanation":"Each layer was separated by straw; sawdust was used on some estates, but straw was more common."}]}]}]},{"id":"lt2","kind":"listening","title":"Listening test 2","sections":[{"title":"Joining a volunteer scheme","context":"Section 1: A woman phones a community volunteering scheme to register for one of its projects.","lines":[{"speaker":"Marcus","text":"Good morning, Kestrel Bay Volunteer Network, Marcus speaking. How can I help?"},{"speaker":"Imogen","text":"Oh, hello. I'm ringing because I'd like to sign up as a volunteer."},{"speaker":"Marcus","text":"Thanks for calling. I can do the registration over the phone now, if you've got ten minutes."},{"speaker":"Imogen","text":"Yes, that's fine."},{"speaker":"Marcus","text":"Lovely. Let's start with your name."},{"speaker":"Imogen","text":"It's Imogen Withers. Withers is W, I, T, H, E, R, S."},{"speaker":"Marcus","text":"W, I, T, H, E, R, S. Thank you. And can I ask how you heard about us?"},{"speaker":"Imogen","text":"Well, I think I first read something about you in the local paper a few weeks ago, but I didn't do anything about it. What actually made me pick up the phone was my neighbour. She's been helping out with you for a year and never stops talking about it."},{"speaker":"Marcus","text":"That's how most people find us. Now, have you got an idea of what sort of volunteering you'd like to do?"},{"speaker":"Imogen","text":"Not really. What are the options?"},{"speaker":"Marcus","text":"At the moment we've got three projects that really need people. There's the food bank in the town centre, sorting donations and packing parcels. There's our driving scheme, taking older people to hospital appointments. And then there's the dune restoration project out at North Sands."},{"speaker":"Imogen","text":"The food bank sounds good. When does that run?"},{"speaker":"Marcus","text":"Weekday mornings, mostly."},{"speaker":"Imogen","text":"Ah, that's no good, I work full time. I did think about the driving, but my car's off the road at the moment. It's being repaired. What's involved with the dunes?"},{"speaker":"Marcus","text":"It's mainly planting grass that holds the sand in place, and putting up fencing to keep walkers off the areas that are recovering. It's outdoor work, fairly physical, but nothing too heavy."},{"speaker":"Imogen","text":"That sounds perfect, actually. Put me down for the dunes."},{"speaker":"Marcus","text":"Great. Dune restoration. The teams go out at the weekend. Which day would suit you better?"},{"speaker":"Imogen","text":"Sunday mornings, I think. Oh, no, wait. Sunday is when I visit my mother, and she lives an hour away. Can I say Saturday instead?"},{"speaker":"Marcus","text":"Saturday mornings it is. That's our bigger group anyway. And how many hours a month do you think you could offer? We ask for a minimum of eight."},{"speaker":"Imogen","text":"Eight seems a bit low. I'd be happy to do ten, if that's all right. Maybe twelve in the summer, but let's say ten for now."},{"speaker":"Marcus","text":"Ten hours. Lovely. Before you start, everyone has to come to an induction session. It covers safety and how the project is run. The next one is on the fourteenth of March, but I'm afraid that one's fully booked. There's another on the twenty-first. Could you make that?"},{"speaker":"Imogen","text":"The twenty-first of March. Yes, that's fine."},{"speaker":"Marcus","text":"Good. Now, clothing. We lend out gloves and all the tools, and we've got waterproof jackets if you need one. The one thing we can't lend is boots, so you'll need to bring your own."},{"speaker":"Imogen","text":"Boots. No problem. Can I ask how it all works? Will I be working on my own? I don't really know much about plants."},{"speaker":"Marcus","text":"Not at all. You'll be in a small team, usually five or six people, with an experienced team leader who'll show you what to do. People often assume we cancel when it rains, but we don't, actually. We only call it off if the wind is very strong, because the sand gets blown everywhere. And I should say we can't pay travel expenses, unfortunately, but there's a free minibus from the railway station on Saturday mornings."},{"speaker":"Imogen","text":"Oh, that's helpful, since I haven't got the car."},{"speaker":"Marcus","text":"And can I ask what made you decide to volunteer? It's just for our records."},{"speaker":"Imogen","text":"Well, a friend told me it would look good on my CV, but I'm not planning to change jobs or anything. And I suppose it'll help me get a bit fitter. But honestly, the main thing is that I've only just moved to the area, and I don't really know anybody yet."},{"speaker":"Marcus","text":"Well, you'll certainly meet people. Right, I think that's everything. You don't need to fill in a registration form, because I've put it all on the computer as we've been talking. What I'll do is email you our safety booklet, and it would be good if you could read that before the induction. You'll get a map of the site on the day."},{"speaker":"Imogen","text":"Great. Thanks so much, Marcus."},{"speaker":"Marcus","text":"Thank you, Imogen. See you on the twenty-first."}],"groups":[{"type":"lform","instructions":"Questions 1–6. Complete the form below. Write ONE WORD AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer.","outline":{"style":"form","rows":[["Kestrel Bay Volunteer Network: registration",""],["First name:","Imogen"],["Surname:","[1]"],["Project chosen:","[2] restoration"],["Available on:","[3] mornings"],["Hours offered per month:","[4]"],["Induction session:","[5] March"],["Must bring own:","[6]"]]},"questions":[{"q":"Gap 1","answer":"Withers","explanation":"She spells her surname W-I-T-H-E-R-S."},{"q":"Gap 2","answer":"dune","alt":["dunes"],"explanation":"The food bank runs on weekday mornings and her car is off the road, so she chooses the dune restoration project."},{"q":"Gap 3","answer":"Saturday","alt":["Saturdays"],"explanation":"She first says Sunday, then changes to Saturday because she visits her mother on Sundays."},{"q":"Gap 4","answer":"10","alt":["ten"],"explanation":"Eight is the minimum and twelve is only a possibility for summer; she says 'let's say ten for now'."},{"q":"Gap 5","answer":"21","alt":["21st","twenty-first","twenty first"],"explanation":"The session on the fourteenth is fully booked, so she will attend on the twenty-first."},{"q":"Gap 6","answer":"boots","explanation":"Gloves, tools and waterproof jackets are lent out; the one thing they can't lend is boots."}]},{"type":"lmcq","instructions":"Questions 7–10. Choose the correct letter, A, B or C.","questions":[{"q":"What finally made Imogen contact the scheme?","options":["A an article in a local newspaper","B a conversation with her neighbour","C a poster at the railway station"],"answer":"B","explanation":"She read about it in the paper first but did nothing; what made her phone was her neighbour."},{"q":"What does Marcus say about the dune project?","options":["A Volunteers work in small groups with a leader.","B Sessions are cancelled when it rains.","C Volunteers' travel costs are paid."],"answer":"A","explanation":"A small team of five or six with a team leader. It continues in rain (only strong wind stops it) and travel expenses are not paid."},{"q":"What is Imogen's main reason for volunteering?","options":["A to improve her CV","B to get fitter","C to meet people in a new area"],"answer":"C","explanation":"CV and fitness are mentioned, but 'the main thing' is that she has just moved and doesn't know anybody."},{"q":"What will Marcus send Imogen?","options":["A a registration form","B a map of the site","C a safety booklet"],"answer":"C","explanation":"No form is needed and the map is given out on the day; he will email the safety booklet."}]}]},{"title":"The new island ferry terminal","context":"Section 2: A ferry company manager tells local residents about a new terminal building and changes to the service.","lines":[{"speaker":"Fiona Garrow","text":"Good evening, everyone, and thank you for coming along. My name's Fiona Garrow, and I'm the operations manager for Corran Island Ferries. As most of you know, the new terminal building here at Port Lennick opens next month, and our timetable changes at the same time, so I'd like to take you through both."},{"speaker":"Fiona Garrow","text":"Let's start with the terminal itself. If you look at the plan on the screen, we're standing at the main entrance, at the bottom. Straight ahead of you, filling the centre of the building, is the waiting lounge, which is about twice the size of the old one."},{"speaker":"Fiona Garrow","text":"The first thing most of you will need is a ticket. The ticket office is immediately on your right as you come through the entrance. In the old building it was on the left, so I expect a few regular passengers will head the wrong way for the first week or two."},{"speaker":"Fiona Garrow","text":"On the other side of the entrance, so on your left as you come in, you'll find the left-luggage lockers. They take coins or cards, and they're big enough for a large suitcase."},{"speaker":"Fiona Garrow","text":"A lot of you asked for somewhere secure to leave bicycles, and I'm pleased to say we've listened. The bicycle storage is along the left-hand wall of the waiting lounge, halfway down, so it's just beyond the lockers as you walk in."},{"speaker":"Fiona Garrow","text":"Directly opposite the bicycle storage, on the right-hand side of the lounge, is the children's play area. We put it there deliberately, so that parents sitting in the lounge can keep an eye on their children."},{"speaker":"Fiona Garrow","text":"If you walk straight across the lounge to the far side, you'll come to the café. It's in the middle of the back wall, and it has big windows looking out over the harbour, so you can watch your ferry coming in while you have a coffee."},{"speaker":"Fiona Garrow","text":"And finally, the first-aid room. On the original plans this was going to be in the top left-hand corner, to the left of the café. But the paramedics asked for it to be nearer the vehicle lanes, so in the end it went into the far right-hand corner, to the right of the café. The top left corner is now just staff offices."},{"speaker":"Fiona Garrow","text":"Right, let me turn to the services. The biggest change is that from next month there'll be an extra sailing on weekday mornings, leaving Port Lennick at six fifteen, which we hope will help those of you who commute to the mainland for work. Many of you also asked about a late-night service at weekends. We looked into it very carefully, but the passenger numbers just didn't add up, so I'm afraid that won't be happening this year."},{"speaker":"Fiona Garrow","text":"In July and August, we'll be bringing in a larger vessel, the Kittiwake, which carries half as many cars again as our usual boat, so the summer queues should be shorter. I know some of you were hoping for a cut in the residents' fare. That's staying exactly where it is, which, given the rise in fuel costs, is quite an achievement. And I'm sorry to say that parking at the terminal won't be free; the daily charge is actually going up slightly, by fifty pence."},{"speaker":"Fiona Garrow","text":"Finally, a few things we'd ask passengers to do. If you're taking a vehicle across, please book your space online at least twenty-four hours before you travel. Turning up without a booking in summer is very risky. You don't need to print your ticket; showing it on your phone is absolutely fine. And you'll no longer need to show photo identification when you board, as that rule has now been dropped."},{"speaker":"Fiona Garrow","text":"If you're bringing a dog, please keep it in the outside deck area. Animals aren't allowed in the passenger lounges on board, apart from assistance dogs. And one last thing: a single ticket now costs exactly half the price of a return, so there's no need to buy a return in advance if you're not sure when you'll be coming back. Now, are there any questions?"}],"groups":[{"type":"lmap","instructions":"Questions 1–6. Label the plan of the new ferry terminal. Choose the correct letter, A–G. You are at the main entrance.","map":{"cols":3,"rows":3,"cells":[{"r":0,"c":0,"t":"A"},{"r":0,"c":1,"t":"B"},{"r":0,"c":2,"t":"C"},{"r":1,"c":0,"t":"D"},{"r":1,"c":1,"t":"Waiting lounge"},{"r":1,"c":2,"t":"E"},{"r":2,"c":0,"t":"F"},{"r":2,"c":1,"t":"Main entrance"},{"r":2,"c":2,"t":"G"}]},"options":["A","B","C","D","E","F","G"],"questions":[{"q":"Ticket office","answer":"G","explanation":"Immediately on your right as you come through the entrance (it used to be on the left)."},{"q":"Left-luggage lockers","answer":"F","explanation":"On the other side of the entrance, on your left."},{"q":"Bicycle storage","answer":"D","explanation":"Along the left-hand wall of the lounge, halfway down, just beyond the lockers."},{"q":"Children's play area","answer":"E","explanation":"Directly opposite the bicycle storage, on the right-hand side of the lounge."},{"q":"Café","answer":"B","explanation":"Straight across the lounge, in the middle of the back wall."},{"q":"First-aid room","answer":"C","explanation":"It was planned for the top left corner (now staff offices) but went into the far right-hand corner, to the right of the café."}]},{"type":"lmcq2","instructions":"Questions 7–10. Choose TWO letters, A–E, for each question.","questions":[{"q":"Questions 7–8. Which TWO changes to the ferry service does Fiona announce?","options":["A an extra early sailing on weekdays","B a lower fare for island residents","C a late-night service at weekends","D a bigger boat during the summer","E free parking at the terminal"],"answer":["A","D"],"explanation":"A 6.15 weekday sailing and the larger Kittiwake in July and August. The weekend night service was rejected, the residents' fare stays the same and parking goes up."},{"q":"Questions 9–10. Which TWO things are passengers asked to do?","options":["A print their tickets before travelling","B book vehicle space at least a day ahead","C carry photo identification","D buy a return ticket in advance","E keep dogs in the outside deck area"],"answer":["B","E"],"explanation":"Book vehicle space online at least twenty-four hours ahead and keep dogs on the outside deck. Phone tickets are fine, ID is no longer needed and singles are half a return."}]}]},{"title":"Planning an ecology field trip","context":"Section 3: Two ecology students, Priya and Owen, discuss their field-trip plan for a disused quarry with their tutor, Dr Hallam.","lines":[{"speaker":"Dr Hallam","text":"Come in, both of you, and sit down. So, you've sent me your plan for the ecology field trip. Let's go through it. First of all, I see you've chosen Carrow Quarry rather than Fenwick Wood. Why was that?"},{"speaker":"Owen","text":"Well, the wood's actually closer to campus, and I'd assumed we'd go there. But Priya looked into it."},{"speaker":"Priya","text":"Yes. There have been dozens of studies in Fenwick Wood already, mostly by students in this department. Carrow Quarry only stopped working about fifteen years ago, and as far as I can tell, almost nobody has looked at how plants have come back there. So we felt we'd have a better chance of finding something new."},{"speaker":"Dr Hallam","text":"That's a good reason. You don't need special permission to work in the wood, so that would have been easier, but I think you've made the right choice. Now, your sampling plan. You've got sixty sample points across the quarry floor."},{"speaker":"Owen","text":"We thought more points would make the results more reliable."},{"speaker":"Dr Hallam","text":"In principle, yes. But you've only got one day on site, and each quadrat takes at least ten minutes if you do it properly. Sixty is far more than you can realistically get through. I'd halve it. Thirty, done carefully, is better than sixty done in a rush. The timing is fine, by the way. Late May is a good point in the season for flowering plants."},{"speaker":"Priya","text":"That makes sense. We were a bit worried about the time ourselves, actually."},{"speaker":"Dr Hallam","text":"What about equipment?"},{"speaker":"Owen","text":"Most of it's straightforward. The department has plenty of quadrats and tape measures. The soil probe was broken last term, but the technician told me it's been replaced."},{"speaker":"Priya","text":"The one thing we weren't sure about is the GPS units. Do we need to book those?"},{"speaker":"Dr Hallam","text":"You do, and that's the thing to watch out for. There are only four of them, and the second-year geographers all want them in May. If you don't reserve them this week, you may well end up without any."},{"speaker":"Owen","text":"I'll book them today. Then there's recording the plant data. I'd suggested using the tablet app the department has, so the data goes straight into a spreadsheet."},{"speaker":"Dr Hallam","text":"It's tempting, but there's no mobile signal at the bottom of the quarry, and the tablet batteries don't last a full day in the field. I've seen whole projects lose their data that way."},{"speaker":"Priya","text":"So paper recording sheets, then? We could take photographs of each quadrat as well."},{"speaker":"Dr Hallam","text":"Paper sheets are the safest option, yes. Photos are a nice extra, as a backup, but don't rely on them for identification; they're rarely clear enough. OK, let's sort out who's doing what, because there's a lot of organising. Who's going to get in touch with the quarry's owner?"},{"speaker":"Owen","text":"That's me. The site belongs to a building company, and I've already found the right person to email."},{"speaker":"Dr Hallam","text":"Good. And the risk assessment?"},{"speaker":"Owen","text":"I said I'd do that as well."},{"speaker":"Priya","text":"Actually, Owen, you've got enough on. I wrote one for my work placement last year, so I know the form. I'll do it."},{"speaker":"Owen","text":"Fine by me."},{"speaker":"Dr Hallam","text":"Then transport. Normally students book the minibus themselves, but the booking system's being changed at the moment, so I'll arrange that through the department office."},{"speaker":"Priya","text":"Thanks. We also want a plant identification guide for the day, just a sheet with pictures of the species we're most likely to find."},{"speaker":"Owen","text":"I'll put that together. I did a module on plant identification last year, so I've got most of the material already."},{"speaker":"Dr Hallam","text":"And what about the soil samples once you get them back to the lab?"},{"speaker":"Priya","text":"I'd like to do the soil analysis. It's the part of the project I'm most interested in."},{"speaker":"Owen","text":"And I'm hopeless with the lab equipment, so that suits me."},{"speaker":"Dr Hallam","text":"Right. The last thing is checking conditions on site the week before. The quarry floor floods after heavy rain, so someone needs to see whether it's safe to go."},{"speaker":"Owen","text":"I could drive out there and have a look."},{"speaker":"Dr Hallam","text":"There's no need. I go past it on my way to work every day, so I'll check it myself and let you both know."}],"groups":[{"type":"lmcq","instructions":"Questions 1–4. Choose the correct letter, A, B or C.","questions":[{"q":"Why did the students choose Carrow Quarry for their field trip?","options":["A It is nearer the university.","B Less research has been done there.","C No special permission is needed."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The wood is closer and needs no permission; the quarry was chosen because almost nobody has studied it."},{"q":"What does Dr Hallam think about the planned number of sample points?","options":["A There are too many to complete in one day.","B More are needed to make the results reliable.","C The number suits the time of year."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Sixty is far more than they can get through in one day; he suggests halving it. Late May relates to timing, not the number."},{"q":"Which equipment problem does Dr Hallam warn the students about?","options":["A The soil probe has not been repaired.","B The quadrats are needed by another group.","C The GPS units may all be taken."],"answer":"C","explanation":"There are only four GPS units and the geographers want them in May. The probe has already been replaced."},{"q":"How will the students mainly record the plant data?","options":["A with a tablet app","B on paper sheets","C by taking photographs"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The app is rejected (no signal, short battery life); photos are only a backup."}]},{"type":"lmatch","instructions":"Questions 5–10. Who will be responsible for each task? Choose the correct letter, A, B or C.","options":["A Priya","B Owen","C Dr Hallam"],"questions":[{"q":"contacting the quarry owner","answer":"B","explanation":"Owen: 'That's me ... I've already found the right person to email.'"},{"q":"writing the risk assessment","answer":"A","explanation":"Owen offered, but Priya takes it over because she has written one before."},{"q":"booking the minibus","answer":"C","explanation":"Students normally book it, but Dr Hallam will arrange it this time."},{"q":"making a plant identification guide","answer":"B","explanation":"Priya suggests it; Owen says he will put it together."},{"q":"analysing the soil samples","answer":"A","explanation":"Priya wants to do the soil analysis."},{"q":"checking the site for flooding","answer":"C","explanation":"Owen offers to drive out, but Dr Hallam passes it daily and will check it himself."}]}]},{"title":"Materials that repair themselves","context":"Section 4: Part of a materials science lecture on self-healing materials.","lines":[{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"Good morning. In today's lecture in our materials science series, I'm going to talk about a family of materials that would have sounded like science fiction not very long ago: materials that can repair their own damage."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"Let's begin with where the idea came from. All engineered materials eventually crack. A bridge, an aircraft wing, a phone case: they all build up tiny cracks, and those cracks grow until, one day, something fails. Living things, on the other hand, deal with damage all the time. Engineers have looked at bone, and at the way trees seal wounds in their bark, but the model that most researchers point to is human skin. When you cut yourself, the body sends materials to the site, a clot forms, and gradually the damage is repaired. The question was whether something similar could be built into a material."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"The goal, I should stress, isn't to make materials that never break. It's to extend the lifespan of structures and products, so that they need replacing less often and maintenance costs fall."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"Let's look first at concrete, the most widely used construction material on the planet. Concrete is strong, but it cracks, and once water gets into those cracks, it corrodes the steel inside. One solution is to mix certain bacteria into the concrete when it's made, together with a food source for them. These bacteria can survive in an inactive state for many years, doing nothing at all. But when a crack forms and water gets in, they wake up. They feed, and as they do so they produce limestone, which gradually fills the crack and seals it."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"Now, you might expect the main use for this to be in bridges. In fact, bridges are relatively easy to inspect and repair. Where the technology really pays off is in tunnels, which are difficult and expensive to inspect, and where a small leak can go unnoticed for years."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"Metals are a rather different story. For a long time it was assumed that metals simply couldn't heal themselves. Then, a few years ago, a research team studying metal fatigue, that is, the weakening caused by repeated stress, noticed something odd under the microscope: some of the tiny cracks they were watching had closed up again. Nobody had been looking for this; it was found entirely by chance. We're still a long way from using it in practice, but it has changed the way we think about metals."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"Finally, I want to spend some time on polymers, in other words plastics, because this is where self-healing technology is most advanced commercially. The best-known approach uses microcapsules, and I'll take you through how it works step by step."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"First, very small capsules are filled with a healing agent, which is essentially a liquid glue, and these capsules are spread evenly through the plastic when it's manufactured. They're tiny, far thinner than a human hair. Then, when a crack spreads through the material, it splits the capsules open. Next, the liquid is drawn into the crack. It isn't pushed in; it moves by what's called capillary action, the same effect that makes water climb up a narrow tube. Once inside the crack, the liquid comes into contact with a catalyst, which has been mixed into the plastic separately. That triggers a chemical reaction, and the liquid hardens, bonding the two faces of the crack together. Tests show that the repaired material can recover up to ninety per cent of its original strength."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"There is a limitation, of course. Once a capsule has been used, it's empty, so the same spot can't be repaired twice. Researchers are now working on networks of tiny channels, rather like blood vessels, that could deliver fresh healing agent again and again. And that will be our topic next week."}],"groups":[{"type":"lnote","instructions":"Questions 1–6. Complete the notes below. Write ONE WORD ONLY for each answer.","outline":{"style":"notes","rows":[["Self-healing materials"],["Background"],["• idea mainly based on the way human [1] repairs itself"],["• aim: to extend the [2] of structures and products"],["Concrete"],["• bacteria stay inactive until [3] enters a crack"],["• bacteria then produce [4], which seals the crack"],["• most useful in [5], because these are hard to inspect"],["Metals"],["• healing was discovered by chance in research on metal [6]"]]},"questions":[{"q":"Gap 1","answer":"skin","explanation":"Bone and bark are mentioned, but 'the model that most researchers point to is human skin'."},{"q":"Gap 2","answer":"lifespan","explanation":"The goal is 'to extend the lifespan of structures and products'."},{"q":"Gap 3","answer":"water","explanation":"When a crack forms and water gets in, the bacteria wake up."},{"q":"Gap 4","answer":"limestone","explanation":"They produce limestone, which fills the crack and seals it."},{"q":"Gap 5","answer":"tunnels","explanation":"Bridges are easy to inspect; the technology really pays off in tunnels."},{"q":"Gap 6","answer":"fatigue","explanation":"A team studying metal fatigue noticed cracks closing by chance."}]},{"type":"lflow","instructions":"Questions 7–10. Complete the flow-chart below. Write ONE WORD ONLY for each answer.","outline":{"style":"flowchart","rows":[["Capsules containing a liquid [1] are spread through the plastic"],["A crack [2] the capsules open"],["The liquid moves into the crack by [3] action"],["The liquid reacts with a [4] and hardens"],["The crack is sealed and up to 90% of the strength returns"]]},"questions":[{"q":"Gap 1","answer":"glue","explanation":"The healing agent is 'essentially a liquid glue'."},{"q":"Gap 2","answer":"splits","explanation":"A crack spreading through the material splits the capsules open."},{"q":"Gap 3","answer":"capillary","explanation":"It isn't pushed in; it moves by capillary action."},{"q":"Gap 4","answer":"catalyst","explanation":"The liquid contacts a catalyst mixed into the plastic, which makes it harden."}]}]}]}];
